window.CNPE_DRILL = [
{"id": "1.1#a-service-resolves-in-dns-but-every-connection-i", "sec": "1.1", "q": "A Service resolves in DNS but every connection is refused. Name the three things you check, in order.", "a": "<p>First, compare the Service selector against the actual pod labels. Second, check pod readiness (unready pods don't join the slice). Third, inspect the EndpointSlice directly. Addresses marked <code>ready: false<\/code> point to a failing readiness probe; an empty slice points to a selector mismatch or unscheduled pods.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#why-does-a-networkpolicy-ipblock-naming-a-servic", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Why does a NetworkPolicy <code>ipBlock<\/code> naming a Service's ClusterIP never work?", "a": "<p>Because DNAT rewrites the ClusterIP to a backend pod IP <em>before<\/em> policy runs. NetworkPolicy operates on pod selectors and identities, not virtual IPs. To allow egress specifically to the API server in Cilium, use <code>toEntities: [kube-apiserver]<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#your-backend-needs-the-real-client-ip-what-do-yo", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Your backend needs the real client IP. What do you change, and what breaks?", "a": "<p>Set <code>externalTrafficPolicy: Local<\/code> on the Service. The trade-off: nodes without a matching pod stop forwarding that traffic, so you now depend on your external load balancer's health checks to route around them, and traffic distribution becomes less even than with <code>Cluster<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#what-is-the-difference-between-a-headless-servic", "sec": "1.1", "q": "What is the difference between a headless Service and a ClusterIP Service with one endpoint?", "a": "<p>A headless Service has no VIP and no datapath translation: DNS hands back pod IPs straight to the client. Even with a single endpoint, a normal ClusterIP Service still routes through the VIP rules, hiding the pod IP. StatefulSets need headless Services for stable per-pod hostnames like <code>pod-0.svc…<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#team-a-can-curl-team-b-s-service-after-you-added", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Team-a can curl team-b's Service after you added an egress allow, but it still fails. What did you forget?", "a": "<p><code>team-b<\/code>'s ingress policy. NetworkPolicies are independent allow-lists at both ends, so both sides have to open up. Also double-check DNS: once <code>team-a<\/code> restricts egress, resolving <code>web.team-b.svc<\/code> requires an explicit port-53 allow to <code>kube-dns<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#an-httproute-applies-cleanly-but-nothing-routes-", "sec": "1.1", "q": "An HTTPRoute applies cleanly but nothing routes. Where do you look first?", "a": "<p>Check <code>status.parents[].conditions<\/code> on the HTTPRoute. <code>Accepted: False<\/code> usually means the Gateway's <code>allowedRoutes<\/code> rejected attachment; <code>ResolvedRefs: False<\/code> means a backend Service or TLS Secret is missing (or lacks a cross-namespace ReferenceGrant). On the Gateway itself, <code>Programmed: False<\/code> means no data plane controller is running, which is always true on this lab's main cluster.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#an-httproute-in-team-a-references-a-service-in-t", "sec": "1.1", "q": "An HTTPRoute in team-a references a Service in team-b. Which object, in which namespace, and what does status say until it exists?", "a": "<p>Create a <code>ReferenceGrant<\/code> in <code>team-b<\/code> (the target namespace) with <code>from<\/code> matching group <code>gateway.networking.k8s.io<\/code>, kind <code>HTTPRoute<\/code>, namespace <code>team-a<\/code>, and <code>to<\/code> matching kind <code>Service<\/code>. Until that grant exists, the route reports <code>ResolvedRefs: False<\/code> (reason <code>RefNotPermitted<\/code>) and returns HTTP 500 for that backend.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#two-backendrefs-carry-weight-3-and-weight-1-what", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Two backendRefs carry weight 3 and weight 1. What split is that, and what happens if one backend Service is missing?", "a": "<p>A 75/25 split, since weights are shares of the total (3 + 1 = 4). If one Service is missing, the route reports <code>ResolvedRefs: False<\/code> (<code>BackendNotFound<\/code>), keeps forwarding traffic to the valid backend, and returns HTTP 500 for the broken backend's share, causing intermittent errors proportional to its weight.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#a-stub-domain-for-corp-example-must-resolve-via-", "sec": "1.1", "q": "A stub domain for corp.example must resolve via 10.0.0.53. Where does the change go and how long before it applies?", "a": "<p>Add a second server block to the <code>coredns<\/code> ConfigMap in <code>kube-system<\/code>: <code>corp.example:53 { errors; cache 30; forward . 10.0.0.53 }<\/code>. The <code>reload<\/code> plugin picks it up within about two minutes with no pod restarts. Verify with <code>nslookup<\/code> from a pod, and check <code>kubectl -n kube-system logs deploy/coredns<\/code> for parse errors if it doesn't take.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.1#which-kube-proxy-mode-is-ga-and-newest-and-what-", "sec": "1.1", "q": "Which kube-proxy mode is GA and newest, and what changes for NodePort when you switch to it?", "a": "<p><code>nftables<\/code>, GA since Kubernetes 1.33 (kernel 5.13+). NodePorts default to <code>--nodeport-addresses primary<\/code>, meaning they only answer on the node's primary IP (other interfaces and localhost stop responding), and kube-proxy stops opening firewall rules for the NodePort range. IPVS is deprecated as of 1.35; iptables remains the default.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#a-node-shows-25-cpu-usage-and-refuses-to-schedul", "sec": "1.2", "q": "A node shows 25% CPU usage and refuses to schedule a pod requesting 200m. Explain, in one sentence.", "a": "<p>The scheduler checks pod <em>requests<\/em> against <em>allocatable<\/em> capacity, never live usage, so the node's CPU requests are already maxed out even though the running pods are mostly idle. Fix it by right-sizing inflated requests (or adding nodes).<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#which-pod-does-the-kubelet-evict-first-under-mem", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Which pod does the kubelet evict first under memory pressure, and why?", "a": "<p>BestEffort goes first because it declared no memory request at all. Next are Burstable pods exceeding their requests, ordered by how far over they are, with Guaranteed pods last. The kernel's <code>oom_score_adj<\/code> mirrors that exact ranking so node-level OOMs choose the same victim.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#your-service-has-p99-latency-spikes-every-few-se", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Your service has p99 latency spikes every few seconds; CPU usage sits at 60% of the limit. First metric you check?", "a": "<p>Check <code>rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total[5m])<\/code>. A comfortable 60% average hides bursty per-period throttling: the container burns its 100ms CFS quota early and freezes until the next period starts.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#why-can-a-vpa-in-auto-mode-and-an-hpa-on-cpu-not", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Why can a VPA in <code>Auto<\/code> mode and an HPA on CPU not coexist on the same workload?", "a": "<p>They fight in a feedback loop: HPA scales replicas off usage divided by requests while VPA keeps rewriting those requests underneath it. The clean fix is running HPA on CPU with VPA in <code>Off<\/code> (advisory) mode, or driving HPA from a custom/external metric while VPA manages CPU/memory.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#a-drain-hangs-forever-on-one-node-what-are-the-t", "sec": "1.2", "q": "A drain hangs forever on one node. What are the two usual causes?", "a": "<p>Either a PodDisruptionBudget with zero allowed disruptions (like <code>minAvailable<\/code> equal to total replicas), or unmanaged bare pods with no controller that <code>kubectl drain<\/code> refuses to evict unless you pass <code>--force<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#is-in-place-pod-resize-usable-on-a-kubernetes-1-", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Is in-place pod resize usable on a Kubernetes 1.37 cluster, and what does the request look like?", "a": "<p>Yes: <code>InPlacePodVerticalScaling<\/code> hit GA in 1.35 (beta/default-on in 1.33). Patch through the <code>resize<\/code> subresource (<code>kubectl patch pod x --subresource resize ...<\/code>), since plain spec patches are rejected as immutable. Watch <code>PodResizePending<\/code> (<code>Infeasible<\/code> / <code>Deferred<\/code>) and <code>PodResizeInProgress<\/code> conditions, and remember QoS class can never change.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#karpenter-refuses-to-consolidate-a-node-you-cons", "sec": "1.2", "q": "Karpenter refuses to consolidate a node you consider empty enough. Where is the reason, and name two things it could be.", "a": "<p>Look for <code>Unconsolidatable<\/code> events on the node. Common blockers include a restrictive PDB, a pod carrying <code>karpenter.sh/do-not-disrupt<\/code>, no cheaper instance replacement available, or an exhausted NodePool disruption budget (Cluster Autoscaler has analogous blockers like <code>safe-to-evict: \"false\"<\/code>, bare pods, and local storage).<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#a-pdb-has-minavailable-2-for-a-2-replica-deploym", "sec": "1.2", "q": "A PDB has minAvailable 2 for a 2-replica Deployment whose pods are CrashLoopBackOff. Drain hangs. Fix without deleting the PDB?", "a": "<p>Patch the PDB with <code>unhealthyPodEvictionPolicy: AlwaysAllow<\/code>. The default <code>IfHealthyBudget<\/code> refuses to evict even unhealthy pods while the budget isn't met, deadlocking the drain. Alternatively, scale the Deployment up temporarily, since deleting the PDB strips protection permanently.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.2#what-is-allocatable-and-which-four-kubelet-setti", "sec": "1.2", "q": "What is allocatable, and which four kubelet settings subtract from capacity?", "a": "<p>Allocatable is the slice of node capacity available for scheduling pods: total capacity minus <code>kubeReserved<\/code>, <code>systemReserved<\/code>, and <code>evictionHard<\/code> thresholds (defaults: <code>memory.available<\/code> 100Mi, <code>nodefs.available<\/code> 10%, <code>nodefs.inodesFree<\/code> 5%, <code>imagefs.available<\/code> 15%). <code>enforceNodeAllocatable<\/code> chooses which cgroups are enforced (default: <code>pods<\/code>).<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#a-pvc-has-been-pending-for-ten-minutes-and-descr", "sec": "1.3", "q": "A PVC has been Pending for ten minutes and <code>describe<\/code> shows no events at all. Bug or not?", "a": "<p>Not a bug if the StorageClass uses <code>WaitForFirstConsumer<\/code> and no pod mounts it yet: until a pod is scheduled, Kubernetes won't provision anything or emit events. It turns into a real bug only once a pod references the claim and it <em>still<\/em> sits Pending, at which point the events appear and tell you why.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#a-pv-sits-in-released-and-your-new-identical-pvc", "sec": "1.3", "q": "A PV sits in Released and your new, identical PVC will not bind to it. Why, and what is the fix?", "a": "<p><code>Retain<\/code> leaves <code>spec.claimRef<\/code> pointing at the old claim, so Kubernetes treats the PV as still spoken for. Clear it with <code>kubectl patch pv &lt;name&gt; -p '{\"spec\":{\"claimRef\":null}}'<\/code> to flip it back to <code>Available<\/code>. The real engineering decision is whether you should wipe the leftover data before letting another workload mount it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#you-delete-a-statefulset-and-its-pvcs-remain-acc", "sec": "1.3", "q": "You delete a StatefulSet and its PVCs remain. Accident or design?", "a": "<p>100% by design. Keeping PVCs alive ensures scaling back up reattaches the same data and prevents an accidental delete from wiping a database. When you want automatic cleanup, opt in via <code>persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy<\/code> (<code>whenDeleted<\/code> and <code>whenScaled<\/code>).<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#two-pods-on-different-nodes-must-share-a-directo", "sec": "1.3", "q": "Two pods on different nodes must share a directory. What do you need, and what will this lab do?", "a": "<p>You need a StorageClass backed by an RWX-capable provisioner (NFS, CephFS, or a cloud file service). Because <code>local-path<\/code> only supports RWO, the claim sits Pending and its events explain why. Saying \"the current provisioner can't do RWX, here's the class/driver swap required\" is the exact right answer both in the lab and on the exam.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#which-quota-lines-cap-tenant-storage-and-why-is-", "sec": "1.3", "q": "Which quota lines cap tenant storage, and why is per-class quota interesting?", "a": "<p><code>requests.storage<\/code> caps total volume size and <code>persistentvolumeclaims<\/code> caps the object count; <code>&lt;class&gt;.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage<\/code> caps a single class. Per-class quotas let platform teams offer an expensive high-IOPS tier without letting every tenant quietly burn budget on it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#you-want-scale-down-to-reclaim-volumes-but-delet", "sec": "1.3", "q": "You want scale-down to reclaim volumes but deleting the StatefulSet by mistake to keep them. Which fields, and since when is this GA?", "a": "<p>Set <code>persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy: { whenScaled: Delete, whenDeleted: Retain }<\/code> (GA since Kubernetes 1.32). Under the hood, <code>Delete<\/code> adds an <code>ownerReference<\/code> to the condemned pod so garbage collection cleans up the PVC once the pod exits; node-failure replacements don't count as scale events and keep their claims.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#an-expansion-to-500gi-failed-because-the-backend", "sec": "1.3", "q": "An expansion to 500Gi failed because the backend has no capacity and the PVC keeps retrying. Can you back out?", "a": "<p>Yes: <code>RecoverVolumeExpansionFailure<\/code> has been GA since 1.34. Just lower the PVC request back down to anything above <code>status.capacity<\/code> (you still can't shrink below current size) and watch <code>status.allocatedResourceStatuses<\/code> and events. Before 1.34, you had to manually flip to Retain, delete the PVC, clear <code>claimRef<\/code>, and recreate it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#restore-a-database-volume-from-last-night-s-snap", "sec": "1.3", "q": "Restore a database volume from last night's snapshot into a new PVC. What does the manifest look like and what must be true first?", "a": "<p>Create a PVC with <code>dataSource: { kind: VolumeSnapshot, name: &lt;snap&gt;, apiGroup: snapshot.storage.k8s.io }<\/code> in the same namespace, using a StorageClass from the same CSI driver and a size at least as large as the snapshot's restore size. Make sure the VolumeSnapshot shows <code>status.readyToUse: true<\/code> first, or the PVC will hang in Pending.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.3#how-do-you-change-a-volume-s-iops-tier-without-r", "sec": "1.3", "q": "How do you change a volume's IOPS tier without recreating it?", "a": "<p>Use a <code>VolumeAttributesClass<\/code> (GA since 1.34). Define the class with your driver's performance parameters, then update the mutable <code>spec.volumeAttributesClassName<\/code> field on the PVC and let the external-resizer apply it. Regular StorageClass parameters, by contrast, are locked at creation time.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#a-tenant-applies-a-deployment-it-is-accepted-and", "sec": "1.4", "q": "A tenant applies a Deployment. It is accepted, and no pods appear. Where is the error?", "a": "<p>One level down on the ReplicaSet. Quotas and PSS are enforced at pod creation, so the Deployment itself applies cleanly while the ReplicaSet logs the rejection in its events and conditions. Run <code>kubectl -n &lt;ns&gt; describe rs -l app=&lt;x&gt;<\/code> or check namespace events. This indirection comes up constantly on the exam.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#why-does-adding-a-limitrange-sometimes-make-prev", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Why does adding a LimitRange sometimes make previously-working pods fail?", "a": "<p>Because LimitRanges enforce <code>min<\/code>/<code>max<\/code> bounds in addition to injecting defaults. Already-running pods stay untouched (admission isn't retroactive), but the next new pod whose explicit requests fall outside those bounds gets rejected with a LimitRange error.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#quota-says-requests-cpu-2-a-tenant-asks-why-they", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Quota says <code>requests.cpu: \"2\"<\/code>. A tenant asks why they cannot run 40 pods when the LimitRange injects 50m each.", "a": "<p>Another quota ceiling hits first: <code>limits.cpu: \"4\"<\/code> divided by the 200m default limit caps them at 20 pods, tying with <code>pods: \"20\"<\/code>. Every line in a ResourceQuota must pass simultaneously, so the tightest limit always wins.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#name-the-isolation-ladder-and-one-cost-of-each-r", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Name the isolation ladder and one cost of each rung.", "a": "<p>Namespaces + quotas + policies (near-zero overhead, but shared kernel and shared API server) → tainted dedicated node pools (isolated kernels, but stranded capacity per pool) → virtual clusters (own API server and CRDs, more control-plane moving parts) → dedicated clusters (strongest boundary, highest operational and idle-capacity cost). Always pick the lightest rung that meets your threat model.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#why-is-services-loadbalancers-1-in-a-tenant-quot", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Why is <code>services.loadbalancers: \"1\"<\/code> in a tenant quota?", "a": "<p>Because cloud LoadBalancers bill continuously whether traffic flows or not. Putting a cap in the tenant quota turns Kubernetes into a spend guardrail, just like per-StorageClass quotas do for expensive disks.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#a-tenant-needs-to-install-its-own-crds-and-admis", "sec": "1.4", "q": "A tenant needs to install its own CRDs and admission webhooks but you cannot afford a cluster per tenant. Which model?", "a": "<p>A virtual control plane per tenant, such as vcluster. Each tenant gets an isolated API server for their own CRDs and webhooks while their pods still share the host cluster's worker nodes through the syncer. Plain namespaces can't isolate cluster-scoped APIs, and full clusters cost too much duplication.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#what-does-hnc-add-to-plain-namespaces-in-two-sen", "sec": "1.4", "q": "What does HNC add to plain namespaces, in two sentences?", "a": "<p>HNC organizes namespaces into parent-child hierarchies so RBAC, NetworkPolicies, and other chosen resources propagate automatically downward, while letting tenant admins spin up subnamespaces without cluster-wide permissions. It also stamps tree labels onto every namespace for team-wide selectors and supports <code>HierarchicalResourceQuota<\/code> across subtrees.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#tenants-keep-launching-pods-with-the-prioritycla", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Tenants keep launching pods with the PriorityClass reserved for platform components. Fix with a quota.", "a": "<p>Add a ResourceQuota in each tenant namespace with <code>scopeSelector: { matchExpressions: [{ scopeName: PriorityClass, operator: In, values: [platform-critical] }] }<\/code> and <code>hard: { pods: \"0\" }<\/code>. Admission immediately rejects any pod claiming that PriorityClass while leaving normal workloads untouched.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.4#why-should-a-storageclass-shared-between-tenants", "sec": "1.4", "q": "Why should a StorageClass shared between tenants use reclaimPolicy Delete?", "a": "<p>Because PVs are cluster-scoped. A <code>Retain<\/code> PV released by one tenant keeps its old data around, and once <code>claimRef<\/code> is cleared, a claim from another namespace could bind to it. <code>Delete<\/code> destroys the backend volume alongside the claim so data can't leak across tenants (or give sensitive tenants their own StorageClasses).<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#a-namespace-runs-almost-no-traffic-and-still-top", "sec": "1.5", "q": "A namespace runs almost no traffic and still tops the cost report. Give the two most likely explanations.", "a": "<p>Either oversized requests (since you're billed on reservation, not actual traffic) or pricey attached objects like LoadBalancers, large PVs, or retained snapshots. Check efficiency first: rock-bottom efficiency points straight at requests, while high efficiency paired with high cost points at attached objects.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#why-is-container-in-that-promql-query-not-option", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Why is <code>container!=\"\"<\/code> in that PromQL query not optional?", "a": "<p>cAdvisor emits per-container series alongside pod-level and node-level rollups with an empty <code>container<\/code> label (and often a pause container labeled <code>container=\"POD\"<\/code>). Without <code>{container!=\"\",container!=\"POD\"}<\/code>, your sum double- or triple-counts the exact same CPU.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#should-idle-cost-be-spread-across-tenants-or-sho", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Should idle cost be spread across tenants or shown separately? Argue both.", "a": "<p>Spread across tenants: teams feel the true cost of the cluster they share, discouraging resource hoarding. Shown separately: bin-packing and node choice belong to the platform team, so keeping an explicit <code>__idle__<\/code> line drives consolidation. Mature orgs show both: per-tenant request spend plus a platform-owned idle row.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#you-cut-a-deployment-s-cpu-request-from-500m-to-", "sec": "1.5", "q": "You cut a deployment's CPU request from 500m to 50m and latency degrades. What happened?", "a": "<p>CPU requests set the cgroup weight, so under node contention your container suddenly gets a tiny slice of CPU time, and if you tightened the limit alongside it, CFS throttling kicks in hard. Right-sizing targets observed peak usage with headroom, not the flat average. Always validate changes with throttling metrics, not just the cost chart.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#give-the-four-step-right-sizing-loop-in-one-brea", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Give the four-step right-sizing loop in one breath.", "a": "<p>Measure usage → compare against requests → adjust requests → confirm zero degradation (no throttling, OOMs, or Pending pods) and check the new efficiency score. That fourth step is what separates real engineering from blind cost-cutting.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#show-cost-per-team-when-teams-span-several-names", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Show cost per team when teams span several namespaces and pods carry a team label. Query?", "a": "<p>Hit <code>/allocation?window=7d&amp;aggregate=label:team<\/code> (or run <code>kubectl cost label --opencost -l team<\/code>). OpenCost aggregates on any <code>label:&lt;key&gt;<\/code> or <code>annotation:&lt;key&gt;<\/code>, and enforcing that label via Kyverno is what keeps your <code>__unallocated__<\/code> bucket small.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#what-does-shareidle-true-change-in-the-numbers-a", "sec": "1.5", "q": "What does shareIdle=true change in the numbers, and who should want it?", "a": "<p>It removes the <code>__idle__<\/code> row and redistributes that idle spend across all other rows proportionally to their cost, per resource. Use it when you want tenants to absorb the full cost of the shared cluster; keep <code>includeIdle=true<\/code> without sharing when the platform team owns bin-packing accountability.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#where-do-on-prem-prices-come-from-and-what-is-th", "sec": "1.5", "q": "Where do on-prem prices come from, and what is the CPU default?", "a": "<p>Nodes without a cloud <code>providerID<\/code> fall back to the <code>custom<\/code> provider and OpenCost's <code>default.json<\/code>: CPU 0.031611/core-hour, RAM 0.004237/GB-hour, GPU 0.95/hour (modeled on GCP us-central1). Override them with a custom pricing ConfigMap or Helm values. Even with notional numbers, the ratios and efficiency scores stay completely valid.<\/p>"},
{"id": "1.5#a-workload-shows-totalefficiency-1-4-is-that-goo", "sec": "1.5", "q": "A workload shows totalEfficiency 1.4. Is that good?", "a": "<p>No: it means actual usage ran 40% above requests. It looks cheap on paper, but the pod is borrowing headroom from neighbors and sits first in line for OOM eviction under memory pressure. Bump requests closer to observed usage; efficiency near (but just under) 1.0 is the sweet spot.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#state-the-four-opengitops-principles-without-loo", "sec": "2.1", "q": "State the four OpenGitOps principles without looking.", "a": "<p>Declarative, versioned and immutable, pulled automatically by agents, and continuously reconciled. If you blank on one under pressure, it's usually \"versioned and immutable\", which also happens to be the one carrying the audit and rollback story.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#why-are-environment-branches-discouraged", "sec": "2.1", "q": "Why are environment branches discouraged?", "a": "<p>Long-lived branches accumulate divergent changes. Promotion turns into messy merges with conflicts and cherry-picks instead of a clean, reviewable diff, and environments drift apart structurally instead of differing only in the values you intended. Directories keep those differences explicit and diffable.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#a-colleague-fixes-production-with-kubectl-edit-d", "sec": "2.1", "q": "A colleague fixes production with <code>kubectl edit<\/code> during an incident. What happens next, and what should they have done?", "a": "<p>Self-heal reverts it on the next reconcile (or flags it as drift if self-heal is off). The right move is to suspend or disable reconciliation for that app, apply the emergency change, then land the same fix in git and resume so it sticks and stays in the audit trail.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#what-does-prune-actually-change-and-what-is-the-", "sec": "2.1", "q": "What does prune actually change, and what is the failure mode in each setting?", "a": "<p>Prune deletes live resources whose manifests disappear from git. With prune off, renamed or removed resources linger forever as invisible cruft. With prune on, deleting a file deletes the running workload, so a careless refactor can cause a production outage. Neither mode is magically safe; safety comes from knowing which one is on.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#where-do-secrets-live-in-a-gitops-repo", "sec": "2.1", "q": "Where do secrets live in a GitOps repo?", "a": "<p>Encrypted (Sealed Secrets or SOPS) or by reference (External Secrets Operator pointing at an external store), never plaintext and never bare base64. The key distinction to state: Sealed Secrets and SOPS keep ciphertext in git, while ESO keeps only a reference and leaves the secret material in the external store.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#what-is-the-rendered-manifests-pattern-and-how-d", "sec": "2.1", "q": "What is the rendered manifests pattern and how does Argo CD 3.5 implement it?", "a": "<p>You render Helm or Kustomize in a pipeline and commit the resulting plain YAML to a branch or directory that the agent syncs from, so reviewers diff final manifests instead of templates. Argo CD's Source Hydrator (beta since 3.5) builds this right into the controller: <code>sourceHydrator.drySource<\/code> is the templated input, and <code>syncSource.targetBranch<\/code>/<code>path<\/code> is where hydrated output gets pushed and synced from using a <code>repository-write<\/code> Secret.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#which-secret-approach-keeps-no-secret-material-i", "sec": "2.1", "q": "Which secret approach keeps no secret material in git at all, and what is its dependency?", "a": "<p>External Secrets Operator. Git holds an <code>ExternalSecret<\/code> referencing keys in a <code>SecretStore<\/code> or <code>ClusterSecretStore<\/code>, and the operator fetches and writes the Kubernetes Secret on a <code>refreshInterval<\/code>. The trade-off is your dependency on that external store and its credentials: if it goes down, rotation pauses (though existing Secrets keep working). Sealed Secrets and SOPS store ciphertext in git instead.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#what-does-flux-bootstrap-actually-create-and-wha", "sec": "2.1", "q": "What does flux bootstrap actually create, and what is the name of the object that then manages Flux itself?", "a": "<p>It installs the controllers, commits <code>gotk-components.yaml<\/code> and <code>gotk-sync.yaml<\/code> under <code>--path<\/code> in your repo, and creates a <code>GitRepository<\/code> and a <code>Kustomization<\/code> both named <code>flux-system<\/code> in the <code>flux-system<\/code> namespace. From then on, that Kustomization reconciles the whole cluster directory (Flux included), so upgrading Flux is just another commit.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.1#why-does-helm-list-show-nothing-for-a-chart-argo", "sec": "2.1", "q": "Why does helm list show nothing for a chart Argo CD deployed?", "a": "<p>Argo CD renders charts using <code>helm template<\/code> semantics and applies the plain manifests itself, so it never creates Helm release Secrets. Flux's helm-controller runs real Helm install and upgrade actions and keeps full release history, which is why <code>helm list -n &lt;storageNamespace&gt;<\/code> sees them. Make sure you call out that difference when comparing engines.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#an-app-is-synced-degraded-what-are-you-allowed-t", "sec": "2.2", "q": "An app is Synced/Degraded. What are you allowed to conclude, and what is the fix?", "a": "<p>Live matches git, so Argo CD did its job; the desired state itself is broken (bad image, impossible resource request, missing config key). Fix it with a git commit. Re-syncing won't change anything because there's no diff left to apply.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#a-sync-fails-with-field-is-immutable-two-ways-fo", "sec": "2.2", "q": "A sync fails with \"field is immutable\". Two ways forward?", "a": "<p>Add <code>Replace=true<\/code> (app-wide or per resource) so Argo uses <code>kubectl replace<\/code> instead of apply, or delete the live resource and let Argo recreate it (<code>Force=true<\/code> automates delete-and-recreate). Pick carefully, though: replace isn't magic. A Service manifest omitting <code>clusterIP<\/code> will still be rejected as immutable, and neither flag can shrink a PVC.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#what-is-an-appproject-for-in-one-sentence-and-na", "sec": "2.2", "q": "What is an AppProject for, in one sentence, and name two things it constrains?", "a": "<p>It's the policy guardrail around a group of Applications, constraining permitted source repos and destination cluster/namespace pairs, plus allowed/denied resource kinds, per-project RBAC roles, and sync windows. It's how you give a team self-serve deployments without letting them deploy anything anywhere.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#you-are-handed-an-applicationset-with-a-git-dire", "sec": "2.2", "q": "You are handed an ApplicationSet with a git directory generator over <code>apps/*/overlays/*<\/code>. How many Applications will exist?", "a": "<p>One per matching directory in the repo at the current revision. Count the directories, then inspect the template's name expression for collisions (two identical names silently overwrite each other). Reading a generator block and predicting the exact output is the exam skill; <code>kubectl -n argocd get applications<\/code> confirms it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#an-app-shows-outofsync-forever-on-a-field-no-hum", "sec": "2.2", "q": "An app shows OutOfSync forever on a field no human edits. What is happening and what fixes it?", "a": "<p>Something inside the cluster is mutating the object after apply: a mutating webhook, an HPA updating replicas, or a defaulting controller. Fix it with <code>ignoreDifferences<\/code> for that path (plus <code>RespectIgnoreDifferences=true<\/code> if needed), or stop managing that field in git. That's a configuration choice, not a controller bug.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#you-changed-the-repo-but-argo-insists-nothing-ch", "sec": "2.2", "q": "You changed the repo but Argo insists nothing changed. Sequence of moves?", "a": "<p>Start with <code>argocd app get --refresh<\/code> (recompare), then <code>--hard-refresh<\/code> (drop the manifest cache). Next, verify the app's <code>targetRevision<\/code> actually points at the branch you pushed to, and finally check <code>repo-server<\/code> logs for auth or render errors. Always try the cheapest check first.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#a-sync-ends-with-operation-phase-error-rather-th", "sec": "2.2", "q": "A sync ends with operation phase Error rather than Failed. What is the difference and where do you look?", "a": "<p><code>Failed<\/code> means the apply reached the API server and was rejected (immutable field, admission denial), with the exact rejection in <code>syncResult.resources[]<\/code>. <code>Error<\/code> means Argo CD itself couldn't complete the operation (render failure, unreachable repo, hook creation failure). For <code>Error<\/code>, check <code>repo-server<\/code> and <code>application-controller<\/code> logs; for <code>Failed<\/code>, read the per-resource message.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#you-must-stop-managing-an-application-but-keep-i", "sec": "2.2", "q": "You must stop managing an Application but keep its workloads running. Exact move?", "a": "<p>Run <code>argocd app delete X --cascade=false<\/code>, or with <code>kubectl<\/code> patch <code>metadata.finalizers<\/code> to strip <code>resources-finalizer.argocd.argoproj.io<\/code> before deleting the Application. Leaving the finalizer in place triggers a foreground cascade delete that wipes every managed resource. For protecting a single resource rather than the whole app, annotate it with <code>Delete=false<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#an-applicationset-with-a-matrix-of-a-git-directo", "sec": "2.2", "q": "An ApplicationSet with a matrix of a git directories generator and a clusters generator: how many Applications, and what breaks if you nest another matrix inside?", "a": "<p><code>directories × clusters<\/code>: one Application per pair, named by your template (watch out for collisions, which silently merge). A matrix takes exactly two child generators and combination generators only nest one level deep, so nesting a matrix inside a matrix inside a matrix gets rejected at generation time rather than by the API server's schema validation.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.2#a-user-can-see-applications-and-sync-them-but-th", "sec": "2.2", "q": "A user can see Applications and sync them but the Logs tab is empty. Which policy line is missing since 3.0?", "a": "<p><code>p, role:&lt;their-role&gt;, logs, get, &lt;project&gt;/*, allow<\/code>. Starting in Argo CD 3.0, logs RBAC is enforced by default and is no longer implied by <code>applications, get<\/code>. That same 3.0 release split child-resource permissions into <code>update/*<\/code> and <code>delete/*<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#flux-has-no-selfheal-setting-why-is-drift-still-", "sec": "2.3", "q": "Flux has no <code>selfHeal<\/code> setting. Why is drift still corrected?", "a": "<p>Because a Kustomization re-applies its rendered manifests on every <code>interval<\/code>; drift correction is built into re-applying, not bolted on as a separate toggle. The trade-off: your drift window equals the interval, and tightening it adds load on the API server.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#a-kustomization-is-ready-false-name-the-three-la", "sec": "2.3", "q": "A Kustomization is Ready False. Name the three layers where it could have failed.", "a": "<p>Source (the <code>GitRepository<\/code> isn't Ready due to auth, ref, or network), build (<code>kustomize build<\/code> failed on a bad path or invalid YAML), or apply/health (the API server rejected a manifest or health checks timed out). The conditions and <code>flux logs<\/code> name the exact layer, so read those before diving into the YAML.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#how-do-you-order-install-crds-then-the-operator-", "sec": "2.3", "q": "How do you order \"install CRDs, then the operator, then the tenant CRs\" in Flux?", "a": "<p>Chain three Kustomizations with <code>dependsOn<\/code>, and set <code>wait: true<\/code> (or explicit <code>healthChecks<\/code>) on each so downstream dependencies wait until resources are actually healthy. Without wait/healthChecks, <code>dependsOn<\/code> only orders the <em>apply<\/em>, not readiness.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#what-is-postbuild-substitute-for-and-what-is-its", "sec": "2.3", "q": "What is <code>postBuild.substitute<\/code> for, and what is its Argo CD analog?", "a": "<p>It injects per-cluster or per-environment variables into manifests right after <code>kustomize build<\/code>, pulling values inline or from ConfigMaps and Secrets. In Argo CD, you'd reach for Kustomize overlays, per-Application Helm values, or ApplicationSet template parameters; Flux just saves you from creating another overlay directory.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#how-does-flux-stop-one-tenant-s-kustomization-fr", "sec": "2.3", "q": "How does Flux stop one tenant's Kustomization from deploying cluster-admin-level resources?", "a": "<p>Set <code>spec.serviceAccountName<\/code> so the controller impersonates that ServiceAccount during apply, capping the tenant's manifests at their own RBAC. Paired with blocking cross-namespace source references, that's Flux's multi-tenancy boundary and the structural counterpart to Argo CD's <code>AppProject<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#a-helmrelease-shows-ready-false-with-reason-roll", "sec": "2.3", "q": "A HelmRelease shows Ready False with reason RollbackSucceeded. Is the release broken or healthy right now?", "a": "<p>The running workload is healthy on the last good revision; the upgrade failed and remediation rolled it back. The HelmRelease stays not-ready because the cluster isn't running the desired new chart or values. Fix the chart or values in git, or if you resolved an out-of-band issue after hitting <code>RetriesExceeded<\/code>, run <code>flux reconcile hr X --reset<\/code> to clear the retry counters.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#a-chart-upgrade-adds-a-crd-field-but-the-cr-that", "sec": "2.3", "q": "A chart upgrade adds a CRD field but the CR that uses it is rejected as unknown. Why, and which field fixes it?", "a": "<p><code>spec.upgrade.crds<\/code> defaults to <code>Skip<\/code> because Helm doesn't upgrade CRDs automatically, leaving the old schema live to prune or reject the new field. Set <code>upgrade.crds: CreateReplace<\/code> (alongside <code>install.crds: CreateReplace<\/code>), or ship CRDs in a separate Kustomization that the HelmRelease depends on.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#make-a-git-push-reconcile-flux-immediately-inste", "sec": "2.3", "q": "Make a git push reconcile Flux immediately instead of at the next interval. Objects and the field you hand to the git server?", "a": "<p>Create a <code>Receiver<\/code> with the right <code>type<\/code> (<code>github<\/code>, <code>gitlab<\/code>, or <code>generic<\/code>; use <code>github<\/code> for Gitea), <code>events: [push]<\/code>, a <code>secretRef<\/code> holding a shared token, and <code>resources<\/code> listing your <code>GitRepository<\/code>. Expose the notification-controller's <code>webhook-receiver<\/code> Service, then configure your git webhook with that host plus <code>status.webhookPath<\/code> and the same secret token.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.3#after-multi-tenant-lockdown-a-tenant-s-kustomiza", "sec": "2.3", "q": "After multi-tenant lockdown, a tenant's Kustomization fails with Forbidden. What did they leave out?", "a": "<p><code>spec.serviceAccountName<\/code>. Once <code>--default-service-account=default<\/code> is enabled, any Kustomization or HelmRelease that omits an explicit account impersonates the namespace's unprivileged <code>default<\/code> ServiceAccount. The API server rejects the apply, and the <code>Ready<\/code> condition shows <code>ReconciliationFailed<\/code> with the Forbidden error. The platform team needs to provision a tenant ServiceAccount and RoleBinding for them to reference.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#two-tasks-in-a-pipeline-run-at-the-same-time-and", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Two tasks in a pipeline run at the same time and you did not expect it. Why?", "a": "<p>There's no <code>runAfter<\/code> and no result dependency linking them, so Tekton runs them concurrently by default. Ordering only comes from an explicit <code>runAfter<\/code> or consuming another task's result - list order in the YAML means nothing.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#how-do-files-get-from-the-clone-task-to-the-buil", "sec": "2.4", "q": "How do files get from the clone task to the build task, and what happens if you bind the workspace to an emptyDir?", "a": "<p>Through a shared workspace backed by a PVC or <code>volumeClaimTemplate<\/code> mounted into both tasks. Because an <code>emptyDir<\/code> lives inside a single pod, each task gets its own brand-new empty directory and the build step sees nothing - a classic \"why is my workspace empty?\" trap.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#where-do-you-put-a-step-that-must-run-whether-th", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Where do you put a step that must run whether the pipeline passed or failed?", "a": "<p>In <code>spec.finally<\/code>. Those tasks run after every main task finishes regardless of outcome, and they can inspect <code>$(tasks.status)<\/code> to branch. Cleanup, notifications, and teardown belong there rather than as a trailing <code>runAfter<\/code> task that gets skipped on failure.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#why-does-the-pipeline-push-to-kind-registry-5000", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Why does the pipeline push to <code>kind-registry:5000<\/code> and not <code>localhost:5001<\/code>?", "a": "<p>Because the build runs inside a pod where <code>localhost<\/code> is the pod's own network namespace. In-cluster pods reach the registry via its CoreDNS name (<code>kind-registry:5000<\/code>), while your host reaches the same store over its published port (<code>localhost:5001<\/code>). Different hostnames, identical registry and digests.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#your-eventlistener-is-ready-and-a-push-produces-", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Your EventListener is Ready and a push produces nothing. Diagnostic ladder?", "a": "<p>Walk the chain in order: did Gitea actually deliver the webhook (check its delivery log)? Did the EventListener receive it (check its pod logs)? Did an interceptor drop it (event type, secret mismatch, or CEL rule)? Did the TriggerBinding extract every field the TriggerTemplate expects (a missing param fails PipelineRun creation)? Each rung leaves its own error.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#a-pipelinerun-shows-status-true-with-reason-comp", "sec": "2.4", "q": "A PipelineRun shows status True with reason Completed rather than Succeeded. What happened?", "a": "<p>The run passed, but one or more tasks were skipped by a <code>when<\/code> expression - check <code>status.skippedTasks<\/code> to see which ones and why. Scripts or graders checking <code>reason == Succeeded<\/code> will trip on this, so check <code>status == \"True\"<\/code> on the <code>Succeeded<\/code> condition instead.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#which-spec-status-value-stops-new-tasks-lets-run", "sec": "2.4", "q": "Which spec.status value stops new tasks, lets running ones finish and still runs finally?", "a": "<p><code>StoppedRunFinally<\/code>. By comparison, <code>CancelledRunFinally<\/code> kills in-flight TaskRuns before running finally, and plain <code>Cancelled<\/code> kills everything and skips finally. <code>PipelineRunPending<\/code> goes the other way - it creates a run parked until you clear the field.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.4#a-step-referencing-a-stepaction-also-sets-image-", "sec": "2.4", "q": "A step referencing a StepAction also sets image. What happens, and why is the design that way?", "a": "<p>Admission rejects it with a validation error: a step using <code>ref<\/code> can't set <code>image<\/code>, <code>command<\/code>, <code>args<\/code>, <code>script<\/code>, <code>env<\/code>, or <code>volumeMounts<\/code>, because the StepAction owns the execution details while the step just wires params and results. That boundary lets a platform team ship one vetted StepAction and let tenants compose Tasks without touching container config.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#you-set-setweight-10-with-3-replicas-and-no-traf", "sec": "2.5", "q": "You set <code>setWeight: 10<\/code> with 3 replicas and no traffic provider. What actually happens?", "a": "<p>Argo Rollouts can only work in whole pods, so it spins up 1 canary pod alongside the stable pods - giving you roughly a 25–33% split instead of 10%. Exact percentages require a traffic router (Istio, Gateway API, NGINX) or a lot more replicas.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#a-canary-aborted-and-the-analysisrun-shows-measu", "sec": "2.5", "q": "A canary aborted and the AnalysisRun shows measurements in phase Error. What do you check?", "a": "<p>Check the metric query and provider first: wrong metric name, no traffic/data yet, unreachable Prometheus, or a label selector matching nothing. Errors burn through <code>consecutiveErrorLimit<\/code> (default 4), so a broken query aborts rollouts just like a broken release - always inspect the measurement error before blaming the app.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#blue-green-with-autopromotionenabled-false-which", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Blue-green with <code>autoPromotionEnabled: false<\/code>: which Service points where, before and after promote?", "a": "<p>Before promotion, the active Service selects the old ReplicaSet's <code>rollouts-pod-template-hash<\/code> while the preview Service selects the new one. After promotion, both select the new hash, and the old ReplicaSet stays warm for <code>scaleDownDelaySeconds<\/code> so rolling back is a single selector flip.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#argo-cd-manages-your-rollout-you-abort-a-bad-can", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Argo CD manages your Rollout. You abort a bad canary. What happens next, and what should you do?", "a": "<p>Because git still names the bad image, Argo CD's next sync re-applies it and kicks off the broken rollout all over again. Revert the commit (or pin the previous tag back) - <code>abort<\/code> stops the bleeding live, while git controls desired state.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#name-two-things-that-make-progressive-delivery-u", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Name two things that make progressive delivery unsafe regardless of tooling.", "a": "<p>Breaking database or API changes that both versions can't handle at the same time (solve with expand-and-contract migrations and backwards-compatible contracts), and metrics too sparse or coarse to catch regressions inside the canary window. Bonus third: an analysis query that accidentally measures the stable pods instead of the canary.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#an-analysisrun-ended-inconclusive-and-the-rollou", "sec": "2.5", "q": "An AnalysisRun ended Inconclusive and the rollout stopped. Did it abort?", "a": "<p>No - an <code>Inconclusive<\/code> run pauses the rollout at its current step and waits for a human to run <code>promote<\/code> or <code>abort<\/code>. It triggers when more than <code>inconclusiveLimit<\/code> samples match neither <code>successCondition<\/code> nor <code>failureCondition<\/code> (note that in Rollouts 1.10, omitting both conditions counts as Successful). Failed aborts, repeated Errors abort, and Inconclusive asks a human.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#flagger-you-set-match-on-a-header-and-also-stepw", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Flagger: you set match on a header and also stepWeight/maxWeight. What runs?", "a": "<p>A/B testing. Whenever Flagger sees an HTTP <code>match<\/code> rule, it ignores <code>maxWeight<\/code> and <code>stepWeight<\/code> and runs <code>iterations<\/code> rounds sending matched traffic to the canary. Drop <code>match<\/code> if you want a weighted canary, or keep only <code>iterations<\/code> for blue-green.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#what-does-progressdeadlineseconds-do-on-a-plain-", "sec": "2.5", "q": "What does progressDeadlineSeconds do on a plain Deployment, and what does it not do?", "a": "<p>After 600 s (default) with no progress, Kubernetes sets <code>Progressing=False<\/code> with reason <code>ProgressDeadlineExceeded<\/code> - the exact condition Argo CD maps to <code>Degraded<\/code>. It does <em>not<\/em> roll back or pause anything, though the old ReplicaSet keeps serving with whatever pods <code>maxUnavailable<\/code> left running. Argo Rollouts' <code>progressDeadlineAbort: true<\/code> is what adds automatic rollback.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.5#why-must-canaryservice-and-stableservice-exist-w", "sec": "2.5", "q": "Why must canaryService and stableService exist when you add trafficRouting to a Rollout?", "a": "<p>Because the traffic router splits requests between two distinct Services, so Rollouts needs one Service whose selector it pins to the canary ReplicaSet and one for stable (by injecting <code>rollouts-pod-template-hash<\/code> into each). Without a router, traffic splits by replica count behind one Service, so neither field is required.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#the-app-is-synced-and-healthy-but-the-feature-we", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"The app is Synced and Healthy but the feature we merged an hour ago is missing.\"", "a": "<p>Bucket 4 (stale). Check for suspension first, then compare the last-sync timestamp and resolved revision against your commit SHA, and confirm the app is tracking the branch you actually pushed to. Trigger a refresh/reconcile before digging deeper.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#sync-fails-service-demo-is-invalid-spec-clusteri", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"Sync fails: <code>Service \"demo\" is invalid: spec.clusterIP: Invalid value: field is immutable<\/code>.\"", "a": "<p>Bucket 2 (cluster refuses). The API server rejected the patch and the sync operation relayed it verbatim. Either fix the manifest in git or use replace/force semantics if the field genuinely needs to change (accepting the recreation churn).<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#argocd-repo-server-logs-show-authentication-requ", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"argocd-repo-server logs show <code>authentication required<\/code>; the workload is untouched and running fine.\"", "a": "<p>Bucket 3 (access). None of your workload pods are involved - the controller's own repo credentials, token scope, or rotated Secret broke. Existing pods keep humming because reconciliation broke, not the running deployment.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#deployment-exists-no-pods-no-error-on-the-deploy", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"Deployment exists, no pods, no error on the Deployment.\"", "a": "<p>Step one level down to the <code>ReplicaSet<\/code>: a ResourceQuota, Pod Security Standards, or an admission webhook blocked pod creation (just like sections 1.4 and 5.3). Delivery succeeded; admission stopped the pods.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#i-keep-deleting-this-configmap-and-it-keeps-comi", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"I keep deleting this ConfigMap and it keeps coming back.\"", "a": "<p>A controller is actively reconciling it - Argo CD self-heal, a Flux Kustomization on its interval, or an operator. Identify the owner via <code>ownerReferences<\/code>, Argo's <code>argocd.argoproj.io/tracking-id<\/code> annotation, or <code>flux trace<\/code>, then fix the source of truth (or suspend reconciliation first if you need a temporary live edit).<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#sync-failed-admission-webhook-validate-kyverno-s", "sec": "2.6", "q": "\"Sync failed: admission webhook \"validate.kyverno.svc-fail\" denied the request\". Which bucket, and where does the fix go?", "a": "<p>Bucket 5 (admission). The manifest reached the API server and Kyverno blocked it. Fix the manifest so it satisfies the rule (usually what the grader expects), or update the policy if the task says the policy itself is wrong. Clicking sync again won't help - Argo CD reports <code>SyncFailed<\/code> and Flux reports <code>ReconciliationFailed<\/code> with the same denial text.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#a-flux-kustomization-reports-artifactfailed-what", "sec": "2.6", "q": "A Flux Kustomization reports ArtifactFailed. What is broken and where do you look first?", "a": "<p>Its upstream source has no valid artifact - the <code>GitRepository<\/code> or <code>OCIRepository<\/code> isn't Ready (<code>AuthenticationFailed<\/code>, <code>GitOperationFailed<\/code>, bad ref) or hasn't fetched yet. That's bucket 3 upstream of the Kustomization: run <code>flux get sources all -A<\/code> and <code>kubectl describe gitrepository<\/code> first. Once the source publishes, the Kustomization heals on its own.<\/p>"},
{"id": "2.6#argo-cd-shows-sync-status-unknown-with-a-compari", "sec": "2.6", "q": "Argo CD shows sync status Unknown with a ComparisonError condition. Bucket?", "a": "<p>Bucket 3 (access/tooling). Comparison itself failed, so Argo CD can't even tell whether live matches git. Common causes are bad repo credentials or URL/revision, a Kustomize/Helm render error in <code>repo-server<\/code>, or an unreachable target cluster. The condition message names the cause, and the workload stays untouched - the signature of bucket 3.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#define-a-platform-in-the-paper-s-own-terms", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Define a platform in the paper's own terms.", "a": "<p>An integrated collection of capabilities, defined and presented according to the needs of its users. That focus on <em>users<\/em> and <em>presentation<\/em> (interfaces) is what separates a real platform from a pile of infrastructure.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#what-is-the-thinnest-viable-platform-and-what-do", "sec": "3.1", "q": "What is the Thinnest Viable Platform, and what does it imply about build-vs-adopt?", "a": "<p>The smallest layer that delivers consistency and speeds up delivery. It pushes you to adopt existing capabilities wherever possible and invest your team's energy in interfaces, integration, and golden paths - the parts nobody can buy off the shelf for your org.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#a-team-can-request-a-namespace-through-a-form-an", "sec": "3.1", "q": "A team can request a namespace through a form, and someone approves it within a day. Is that self-service?", "a": "<p>No. Self-service means automated fulfillment with no human in the loop. A web form backed by a manual approver is just a nicer skin over a ticket queue - operational maturity, not scalable. Encode guardrails into the API so fulfillment is automatic, reserving manual approvals for genuine regulatory gates.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#name-the-dora-four-and-one-platform-specific-mea", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Name the DORA four and one platform-specific measure the paper adds.", "a": "<p>The DORA four are deployment frequency, lead time for changes, time to restore service, and change failure rate. On top of those, the paper adds platform-specific measures like request-to-fulfillment latency for a capability, time to first contribution, and active adoption - measuring the platform itself rather than just the apps running on it.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#which-platform-attribute-does-a-mandatory-non-co", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Which platform attribute does a mandatory, non-composable platform violate, and why does it matter practically?", "a": "<p>\"Optional and composable.\" Practically, mandated platforms hide their own flaws: instead of giving honest feedback that improves the product, teams quietly route around them. Making adoption optional isn't just polite - it's your clearest feedback signal.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#list-the-five-benefits-the-white-paper-says-ente", "sec": "3.1", "q": "List the five benefits the white paper says enterprises get from investing in platforms.", "a": "<p>First, reducing cognitive load on product teams to speed up delivery. Second, improving reliability and resiliency by putting dedicated experts on platform capabilities. Third, accelerating delivery by reusing shared tools and knowledge across teams. Fourth, reducing security, regulatory, and functional risk through governance. Fifth, getting more out of public cloud and managed services by delegating implementation while keeping control over user experience.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#name-the-five-aspects-and-four-levels-of-the-pla", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Name the five aspects and four levels of the Platform Engineering Maturity Model.", "a": "<p>The five aspects are Investment, Adoption, Interfaces, Operations, and Measurement; the four levels are Provisional, Operational, Scalable, and Optimizing. Each aspect is scored independently, and the model explicitly notes that Level 4 isn't a blanket goal because every step up costs more budget and time.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#which-glossary-role-decides-what-the-platform-sh", "sec": "3.1", "q": "Which glossary role decides what the platform should do, and which builds it?", "a": "<p>The <strong>platform product manager<\/strong> researches user needs, owns the roadmap, and guides the team, while the <strong>platform engineer<\/strong> builds and maintains the interfaces and tools to meet those requirements. Capability providers (internal teams or vendors) run the underlying services, and platform users are whoever runs workloads on top.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.1#an-organization-mandates-the-platform-for-produc", "sec": "3.1", "q": "An organization mandates the platform for production releases and runs office hours because users cannot find features. Which aspect and level?", "a": "<p>Adoption at Level 2 (Operational): extrinsic push via mandates and incentives, fragmented usage, and help-desk style office hours. The next rung is Level 3 (intrinsic pull): great docs, ergonomic interfaces, shared roadmaps, and feedback channels that make teams adopt the platform because it genuinely helps them.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#a-user-says-their-field-does-not-work-kubectl-ge", "sec": "3.2", "q": "A user says their field \"does not work\". <code>kubectl get -o yaml<\/code> shows it missing entirely, and no error was printed. What happened?", "a": "<p>Schema pruning. The field isn't declared in the CRD's OpenAPI schema, so the API server stripped it on write - and whatever applied it bypassed strict field validation (a controller, a GitOps sync, or <code>--validate=false<\/code>), which is why nothing errored. Fix it by adding the field to the schema (or, in rare cases, adding <code>x-kubernetes-preserve-unknown-fields<\/code> on that subtree). Declaring it properly gives users real validation and docs.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#your-controller-s-status-writes-vanish-one-likel", "sec": "3.2", "q": "Your controller's status writes vanish. One likely cause?", "a": "<p>The <code>status<\/code> subresource is enabled, and the writer is hitting the main resource endpoint - which silently ignores the <code>status<\/code> stanza. Switch to <code>UpdateStatus()<\/code> (or <code>kubectl patch --subresource=status<\/code>). And watch out for the flip side: with no status subresource at all, any spec write from a user overwrites the status your controller just wrote.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#you-must-forbid-changing-spec-tier-after-creatio", "sec": "3.2", "q": "You must forbid changing <code>spec.tier<\/code> after creation. How, without a webhook?", "a": "<p>Add a CEL transition rule right on the field using <code>x-kubernetes-validations<\/code>: <code>rule: \"self == oldSelf\"<\/code> with a clear message like <code>\"tier is immutable\"<\/code>. Because the API server evaluates CEL against the stored object on updates, you get immutability with zero webhooks to operate.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#you-want-to-serve-v1alpha1-and-v1beta1-with-diff", "sec": "3.2", "q": "You want to serve <code>v1alpha1<\/code> and <code>v1beta1<\/code> with different fields. What do you need?", "a": "<p>Mark both versions <code>served: true<\/code>, mark exactly one <code>storage: true<\/code>, and set up a conversion strategy: <code>None<\/code> only works when fields are compatible, so diverging fields need a conversion <code>Webhook<\/code>. Everything stays persisted in the storage version in etcd and gets converted on the fly when served.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#what-does-a-crd-give-you-for-free-and-what-does-", "sec": "3.2", "q": "What does a CRD give you for free, and what does it definitively not give you?", "a": "<p>For free: etcd persistence, OpenAPI and CEL validation, defaulting, RBAC integration, watches and informers, <code>kubectl<\/code> support and <code>explain<\/code> docs, ResourceQuota counting, and admission/policy visibility. Definitely not free: behavior. Nothing actually happens until a controller reconciles the object.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#your-cel-rule-over-a-list-of-strings-is-rejected", "sec": "3.2", "q": "Your CEL rule over a list of strings is rejected with \"exceeded budget by more than 100x\". What do you change?", "a": "<p>Bound the inputs your rule loops over: add <code>maxItems<\/code> on the array and <code>maxLength<\/code> on its string items (or <code>maxProperties<\/code> on a map). The static cost estimator assumes worst-case sizes for unbounded collections, so adding bounds lets the exact same CEL expression pass - and gives your API cleaner input limits too.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#you-tightened-a-pattern-on-spec-name-existing-ob", "sec": "3.2", "q": "You tightened a pattern on spec.name. Existing objects with old-style names must still be updatable. What makes that work, and what does it not cover?", "a": "<p>Validation ratcheting (GA since 1.33): updates succeed as long as every part failing the new schema stays unchanged. It doesn't cover transition rules (<code>oldSelf<\/code>), list-type changes, new <code>required<\/code> fields, renamed properties, metadata rules, or create requests. For custom grandfathering rules, use <code>optionalOldSelf: true<\/code> inside a CEL rule.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#you-want-to-drop-v1alpha1-from-a-crd-but-the-upd", "sec": "3.2", "q": "You want to drop v1alpha1 from a CRD but the update is refused. Why, and what is the sequence?", "a": "<p><code>status.storedVersions<\/code> still includes <code>v1alpha1<\/code>, so etcd might still hold objects serialized in that version and Kubernetes refuses to strand them. Set <code>v1<\/code> as the storage version (optionally setting <code>served: false<\/code> on <code>v1alpha1<\/code> first), rewrite every object via a <code>StorageVersionMigration<\/code> or a no-op update loop so etcd re-persists them as <code>v1<\/code>, patch <code>status.storedVersions<\/code> to <code>[\"v1\"]<\/code>, and then remove <code>v1alpha1<\/code> from <code>spec.versions<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.2#enable-kubectl-get-widgets-field-selector-spec-t", "sec": "3.2", "q": "Enable kubectl get widgets --field-selector spec.tier=gold. Field, version status, and limits?", "a": "<p>Add <code>spec.versions[].selectableFields: [{jsonPath: .spec.tier}]<\/code> (GA since Kubernetes 1.32). You're limited to scalar fields, at most 8 per version, and clients have to query a version that declares them. It eliminates the old workaround of duplicating spec fields into labels just to filter efficiently.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#you-patch-a-cr-and-its-status-still-reports-the-", "sec": "3.3", "q": "You patch a CR and its status still reports the old problem. What do you check before believing it?", "a": "<p>Compare <code>status.observedGeneration<\/code> against <code>metadata.generation<\/code>. If they don't match, the controller hasn't reconciled your edit yet and status is still reporting on the previous spec. Once they match and the error persists, you're looking at a real current failure.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#a-namespace-has-been-terminating-for-twenty-minu", "sec": "3.3", "q": "A namespace has been Terminating for twenty minutes. Method?", "a": "<p>List whatever objects remain (<code>kubectl api-resources --verbs=list --namespaced -o name | xargs -n1 kubectl get -n &lt;ns&gt;<\/code>), check the finalizer holding them, and verify whether the owning controller is alive. Reviving the controller is the clean fix; stripping the finalizer by hand is a last resort that leaks whatever teardown it was guarding.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#why-is-deleting-an-operator-managed-pod-not-a-wa", "sec": "3.3", "q": "Why is deleting an operator-managed pod not a way to \"reset\" anything?", "a": "<p>Because reconciliation is level-based: the controller simply sees a missing pod and spins up a replacement, with zero memory of why you deleted it. To change behavior, fix the spec or underlying cause - otherwise the reconcile loop will faithfully restore the same state forever.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#an-operator-s-crs-apply-fine-and-nothing-happens", "sec": "3.3", "q": "An operator's CRs apply fine and nothing happens at all. Two checks?", "a": "<p>First, verify the controller pod is running and actually holds its leader Lease. Second, check its logs for RBAC <code>Forbidden<\/code> errors covering your namespace or kind - the classic silent failure. Only after clearing both should you check whether the CR is missing a selector, class field, or has a pause annotation.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#name-the-five-ways-an-operator-integrates-with-t", "sec": "3.3", "q": "Name the five ways an operator integrates with the rest of a platform.", "a": "<p>Its CRs are delivered through GitOps, its metrics are scraped and alerted on, its connection Secrets are consumed by apps (or bridged via ESO), its CRs are validated by admission policy, and its API is wrapped behind a thinner developer-facing abstraction (Crossplane, kro, or a portal template). That's the full \"integration\" half of the competency.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#an-operator-deployment-has-three-replicas-and-on", "sec": "3.3", "q": "An operator Deployment has three replicas and only one does work. How does it decide, and how do you see which?", "a": "<p>Leader election via a <code>Lease<\/code> in the operator's namespace (named by <code>LeaderElectionID<\/code>, defaulting to a 15 s lease and 10 s renew deadline). Run <code>kubectl -n &lt;ns&gt; get lease &lt;id&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.holderIdentity}'<\/code> to identify the active pod. Standby replicas warm their caches without reconciling, so checking the wrong pod's logs gives you nothing.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#what-does-owns-in-a-controller-builder-do-and-wh", "sec": "3.3", "q": "What does Owns() in a controller builder do, and what does it explain about operator behavior you see in the cluster?", "a": "<p><code>Owns()<\/code> watches a child resource kind and, whenever a child changes, enqueues its parent CR using the controller <code>ownerReference<\/code>. That's why deleting an operator-owned Deployment or Secret triggers an immediate recreate, why <code>ownerReferences<\/code> map an operator's entire object tree, and why operators require full RBAC on every child kind they create.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#install-an-operator-with-olm-v1-pinned-to-versio", "sec": "3.3", "q": "Install an operator with OLM v1 pinned to versions below 2.0 from channel stable. Which object, and what does upgradeConstraintPolicy: SelfCertified change?", "a": "<p>Create a <code>ClusterExtension<\/code> naming <code>spec.namespace<\/code>, <code>spec.serviceAccount.name<\/code> (an SA you pre-configured with the bundle's RBAC), and <code>spec.source.catalog: { packageName, channels: [stable], version: \"&lt;2.0\" }<\/code>. The default <code>CatalogProvided<\/code> policy only permits upgrades on the catalog's upgrade graph; <code>SelfCertified<\/code> lets you jump to any version (including downgrades), while CRD upgrade safety checks still run unless disabled.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.3#why-should-a-condition-be-named-ready-or-degrade", "sec": "3.3", "q": "Why should a condition be named Ready or Degraded rather than Deploying, and what is polarity?", "a": "<p>Condition types describe an observed state (adjective or past participle), while ongoing transitions are represented by toggling status (like <code>Reconciling=True<\/code> rather than <code>Deploying<\/code>). Polarity tells you whether <code>True<\/code> is healthy (<code>Ready<\/code>) or unhealthy (<code>Degraded<\/code>, <code>Stalled<\/code>) - and because generic tooling can't guess polarity for every custom condition, the convention provides a roll-up <code>Ready<\/code> summary condition.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#which-template-type-creates-kubernetes-objects-a", "sec": "3.4", "q": "Which template type creates Kubernetes objects, and what is its idempotency story?", "a": "<p><code>resource<\/code>. With <code>action: create<\/code>, a re-run fails on <code>AlreadyExists<\/code>; with <code>action: apply<\/code>, it converges cleanly. Because Workflows don't reconcile, you have to design for duplicate submissions yourself using apply semantics or an existence guard.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#a-workflow-node-says-forbidden-where-do-you-look", "sec": "3.4", "q": "A workflow node says <code>forbidden<\/code>. Where do you look, and what is the classic first offender?", "a": "<p>Check the workflow's ServiceAccount and its RBAC bindings, not the workflow spec. The classic first offender in Argo is missing <code>create,patch<\/code> on <code>workflowtaskresults.argoproj.io<\/code>, the executor's own reporting channel, which blows up before your intended resource permission is even tried.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#when-is-a-workflow-the-wrong-tool-and-what-repla", "sec": "3.4", "q": "When is a workflow the wrong tool, and what replaces it?", "a": "<p>Whenever the desired state has to stay true indefinitely. A workflow runs once and walks away. Reach for a controller or operator, or a declarative composition engine like Crossplane or kro that keeps reconciling. Workflows are for sequences with a finish line.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#two-dag-tasks-with-no-dependencies-what-happens-", "sec": "3.4", "q": "Two DAG tasks with no <code>dependencies<\/code>: what happens, and how do you gate one on a condition?", "a": "<p>They run in parallel. Use <code>dependencies<\/code> (or <code>depends<\/code>) for ordering and <code>when<\/code> for conditional branching against parameters or an earlier task's result. Putting a validation node at the root that everything else depends on is the standard \"fail before side effects\" pattern.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#what-turns-a-workflow-into-a-self-service-produc", "sec": "3.4", "q": "What turns a Workflow into a self-service product?", "a": "<p>Promoting it to a <code>WorkflowTemplate<\/code> with typed, documented, defaulted parameters, a clean submit surface (UI, CLI, portal, or Backstage action), input validation before side effects, and an auditable record of who ran what. Same logic underneath, packaged as an interface.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#a-step-must-run-after-build-whether-build-succee", "sec": "3.4", "q": "A step must run after <code>build<\/code> whether <code>build<\/code> succeeded or failed, but only if <code>lint<\/code> succeeded. How do you express that in a DAG?", "a": "<p>Switch from <code>dependencies<\/code> to <code>depends<\/code>: <code>depends: \"(build.Succeeded || build.Failed) &amp;&amp; lint.Succeeded\"<\/code>. Those outcome operands (<code>.Succeeded<\/code>, <code>.Failed<\/code>, <code>.Errored<\/code>, <code>.Skipped<\/code>, <code>.Omitted<\/code>, <code>.Daemoned<\/code>) unlock outcome-based ordering, whereas <code>dependencies<\/code> can only express \"after it succeeded\".<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#two-provisioning-runs-for-the-same-tenant-must-n", "sec": "3.4", "q": "Two provisioning runs for the same tenant must never execute concurrently, and a nightly job must skip its tick if the previous one is still running. Fields?", "a": "<p>Use a workflow-level <code>synchronization.mutexes<\/code> entry named per tenant (or a ConfigMap-backed <code>semaphores<\/code> entry of size 1) to serialize runs within a namespace. For the schedule, set <code>CronWorkflow.spec.concurrencyPolicy: Forbid<\/code> (<code>Replace<\/code> would kill the active run instead). A waiting Workflow sits in <code>Pending<\/code> with a message naming the lock, not a scheduling error.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#you-submit-a-workflow-with-serviceaccountname-se", "sec": "3.4", "q": "You submit a Workflow with <code>serviceAccountName<\/code> set and the controller rejects it, although the SA exists. Likely cause?", "a": "<p>The controller is running with <code>workflowRestrictions.templateReferencing: Strict<\/code> or <code>Secure<\/code>. Under either mode, submissions must use <code>workflowTemplateRef<\/code> and may only override an allow-listed set of fields (<code>arguments<\/code>, <code>entrypoint<\/code>, GC settings, and a few others). Security-sensitive fields like <code>serviceAccountName<\/code>, <code>volumes<\/code>, or <code>podSpecPatch<\/code> belong in the <code>WorkflowTemplate<\/code>. Move the field there and resubmit.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.4#what-does-an-exit-handler-see-that-a-normal-temp", "sec": "3.4", "q": "What does an exit handler see that a normal template does not, and what is the difference between <code>argo stop<\/code> and <code>argo terminate<\/code>?", "a": "<p>An <code>onExit<\/code> template (or <code>hooks.exit<\/code>) runs after the entrypoint finishes and can read <code>{{workflow.status}}<\/code> (<code>Succeeded<\/code>, <code>Failed<\/code>, <code>Error<\/code>) and <code>{{workflow.failures}}<\/code>, letting it branch with <code>when<\/code>. <code>argo stop<\/code> halts the running nodes and still runs exit handlers, while <code>argo terminate<\/code> kills everything immediately and skips them. Keep that distinction in mind whenever cleanup logic depends on an exit hook.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#name-the-three-objects-and-who-owns-each", "sec": "3.5", "q": "Name the three objects and who owns each.", "a": "<p><strong>XRD<\/strong> (platform team: the promised API and schema), <strong>Composition<\/strong> (platform team: the expansion recipe, safe to evolve without changing the API), and <strong>XR<\/strong> (developer: an instance). Crossplane generates the CRD for you, which is the main labor saving.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#an-xr-is-stuck-not-ready-and-the-message-is-unhe", "sec": "3.5", "q": "An XR is stuck not-Ready and the message is unhelpful. Diagnostic order?", "a": "<p>Check packages first (make sure <code>providers<\/code> and <code>functions<\/code> are Installed and Healthy), run <code>crossplane resource trace<\/code>, then inspect the composed resources' own conditions and the underlying API error inside them. Always work bottom-up, because that's the direction failures propagate.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#why-can-a-namespaced-xr-not-compose-a-cluster-sc", "sec": "3.5", "q": "Why can a namespaced XR not compose a cluster-scoped resource directly?", "a": "<p>Kubernetes ownership rules forbid a namespaced object from owning a cluster-scoped one, and Crossplane uses <code>ownerReferences<\/code> to manage lifecycles. The workaround used in this lab is a namespaced <code>Object<\/code> (<code>kubernetes.m.crossplane.io<\/code>) that <em>wraps<\/em> the cluster-scoped manifest, authenticated with a <code>ClusterProviderConfig<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#you-edit-a-composition-what-happens-to-the-twelv", "sec": "3.5", "q": "You edit a Composition. What happens to the twelve XRs already using it?", "a": "<p>All twelve reconcile to the new recipe automatically, without anyone touching them. That's hugely powerful, and it's also a wide blast radius. When you need controlled rollouts, set <code>compositionUpdatePolicy: Manual<\/code> to pin instances to a revision, or version the Composition and migrate tenants deliberately.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#when-would-you-choose-kro-or-a-plain-operator-ov", "sec": "3.5", "q": "When would you choose kro or a plain operator over Crossplane?", "a": "<p>Pick <strong>kro<\/strong> when you want the same declarative in-cluster expansion with far less machinery and can accept a younger API (3.6). Pick a <strong>plain operator<\/strong> when the value is ongoing domain behavior (failover, backups, upgrade orchestration) rather than wiring existing resources together. Crossplane shines when you're composing many resources, especially across external cloud APIs, behind one clean abstraction.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#an-xr-shows-synced-true-ready-false-another-show", "sec": "3.5", "q": "An XR shows <code>Synced=True<\/code>, <code>Ready=False<\/code>. Another shows <code>Synced=False<\/code>. What is each telling you?", "a": "<p><code>Synced=False<\/code> means Crossplane couldn't render or apply the composition (unknown function, bad patch path, forbidden, invalid manifest); the XR's message names the error, and the composed resources may not even exist yet. <code>Synced=True, Ready=False<\/code> means the recipe applied cleanly, but a composed resource isn't ready yet, so descend into its conditions (<code>crossplane resource trace<\/code> prints the whole tree). The first is usually a platform-team bug; the second is usually the provider or external system.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#you-must-import-an-existing-cloud-database-into-", "sec": "3.5", "q": "You must import an existing cloud database into Crossplane without risking any change to it. Fields?", "a": "<p>Create a managed resource with <code>spec.managementPolicies: [\"Observe\"]<\/code> and set the <code>crossplane.io/external-name<\/code> annotation to the real database's identifier. Crossplane populates <code>status.atProvider<\/code> and never creates, updates, or deletes anything. Later, you can widen the policy (adding <code>Update<\/code> and <code>Delete<\/code>) to take over management gradually.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#which-one-field-on-the-xrd-makes-every-instance-", "sec": "3.5", "q": "Which one field on the XRD makes every instance use one specific Composition regardless of what the XR asks for, and what happens to <code>compositionRef<\/code> on the XR?", "a": "<p><code>spec.enforcedCompositionRef.name<\/code>. It overrides <code>compositionRef<\/code>, <code>compositionSelector<\/code>, and <code>defaultCompositionRef<\/code>, and Crossplane overwrites the XR's <code>compositionRef<\/code> with the enforced Composition. By contrast, <code>defaultCompositionRef<\/code> only fills in a blank at creation time and never overrides an explicit choice.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.5#a-provider-shows-healthy-false-name-the-three-us", "sec": "3.5", "q": "A provider shows <code>HEALTHY: False<\/code>. Name the three usual causes and where each is visible.", "a": "<p><strong>Image pull failure<\/strong> (a private registry missing <code>packagePullSecrets<\/code>): check the revision's conditions and provider pod events. <strong>Unsatisfied dependency or Crossplane version constraint<\/strong>: check the <code>ProviderRevision<\/code> condition reason <code>UnhealthyPackageRevision<\/code> and the <code>Lock<\/code> object. <strong>Crashing provider pod<\/strong>: standard pod debugging in <code>crossplane-system<\/code>. Remember that if the package is healthy while MRs show <code>Synced=False<\/code> with <code>forbidden<\/code>, you're looking at RBAC, not a broken package.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#how-does-kro-know-to-create-the-namespace-before", "sec": "3.6", "q": "How does kro know to create the namespace before the quota?", "a": "<p>From the CEL reference: the quota template reads <code>${ns.metadata.name}<\/code>, so kro builds a dependency graph automatically and orders creation accordingly. You never write <code>dependsOn<\/code>, which also means a refactor that drops a reference can silently reorder your graph.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#a-team-asks-for-a-portal-so-developers-can-self-", "sec": "3.6", "q": "A team asks for \"a portal so developers can self-serve\". What do you ask before agreeing?", "a": "<p>Ask what actually happens after the user clicks submit. If fulfillment behind the scenes is still manual, the portal is just a nicer ticket queue. The real platform work is the automated chain underneath (templates → git → GitOps → controllers), and the portal is the last 10%. That's the white paper's interface-versus-capability distinction.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#nightly-image-rebuild-and-rescan-which-engine-an", "sec": "3.6", "q": "Nightly image rebuild-and-rescan: which engine, and why not the others?", "a": "<p>A <code>CronWorkflow<\/code>: it's scheduled, runs to completion, and gives you a DAG with retries and notifications. You wouldn't pick an operator (nothing to reconcile continuously), Tekton (no git event trigger, though it could execute the build), or Crossplane (no persistent resource graph to maintain).<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#give-the-three-questions-that-decide-the-engine", "sec": "3.6", "q": "Give the three questions that decide the engine.", "a": "<p>Does the state need to reconcile forever or run once? What's the trigger: an API object, a schedule, a git event, or a human? And who's the audience: a YAML author, a form filler, or another system? Answer those three and the decision table practically fills itself in.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#what-does-the-scm-provider-generator-actually-wa", "sec": "3.6", "q": "What does the SCM-provider generator actually watch, and how would you debug a repo that never appears?", "a": "<p>It polls the git host's API for repos in an org matching your filters (name pattern, topic, or required path), then stamps out one Application per match. Debug by checking the generator block (org, filters, credentials Secret), then reading the ApplicationSet's status conditions and controller logs for API errors, which usually trace back to a token scope or a filter mismatch.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#an-rgd-is-active-but-every-instance-sits-with-re", "sec": "3.6", "q": "An RGD is <code>Active<\/code>, but every instance sits with <code>ResourcesReady=False<\/code> and the kro controller logs <code>forbidden<\/code>. Cause?", "a": "<p>kro is running in RBAC <code>aggregation<\/code> mode and nobody granted its controller permissions on the kind your RGD templates. Because static analysis still passes, the RGD goes <code>Active<\/code>, and the error only shows up when the controller tries to create child objects. Add a ClusterRole for that kind labeled <code>rbac.kro.run/aggregate-to-controller: \"true\"<\/code>. Same failure family as a Crossplane provider missing RBAC or an Argo Workflows SA missing <code>workflowtaskresults<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#a-backstage-template-s-publish-step-fails-with-a", "sec": "3.6", "q": "A Backstage template's publish step fails with an authentication error although the form rendered fine. Where do you look, and what is the other common form-time failure?", "a": "<p>Check the scaffolder backend's integration config (<code>integrations.github|gitlab|gitea<\/code> in <code>app-config.yaml<\/code>) and verify its token actually has repo-creation rights in the target org. The classic form-time failure is a <code>RepoUrlPicker<\/code> whose <code>allowedHosts<\/code> list doesn't include your git host, blocking submission before any step ever runs.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#how-do-you-make-a-kro-resource-wait-for-a-deploy", "sec": "3.6", "q": "How do you make a kro resource wait for a Deployment to actually be available before creating the Service that points at it, and why is the default insufficient?", "a": "<p>Add <code>readyWhen: [${deployment.status.availableReplicas &gt; 0}]<\/code> (or check its <code>Available<\/code> condition) on the Deployment entry. By default, kro considers a resource ready the moment it exists and its CEL expressions resolve, so dependents get created even if the pods never start. Don't confuse it with <code>includeWhen<\/code>, which decides whether to create the resource at all.<\/p>"},
{"id": "3.6#which-backstage-kinds-must-a-catalog-contain-bef", "sec": "3.6", "q": "Which Backstage kinds must a catalog contain before <code>spec.owner: group:default/team-x<\/code> resolves, and what happens if they are missing?", "a": "<p>A <code>Group<\/code> named <code>team-x<\/code> (typically alongside <code>User<\/code> entities linked via <code>memberOf<\/code>). If it's missing, the Component still loads into the catalog, but its <code>ownedBy<\/code> relation dangles: the UI renders a broken owner link and ownership filters won't find it. In practice, Groups and Users are synced from an org provider like GitHub or LDAP rather than written by hand.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#your-servicemonitor-is-correct-and-no-target-app", "sec": "4.1", "q": "Your ServiceMonitor is correct and no target appears. List the checks in order.", "a": "<p>1) Prometheus's <code>serviceMonitorSelector<\/code> and <code>serviceMonitorNamespaceSelector<\/code>: is Prometheus even watching your monitor? 2) Does the monitor's selector match the Service's labels (and its <code>namespaceSelector<\/code> match the Service's namespace)? 3) Is <code>endpoints[].port<\/code> the Service's port <em>name<\/em>? 4) Does the Service have ready endpoints? 5) Check RBAC and NetworkPolicy between <code>monitoring<\/code> and the target namespace.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#why-is-sum-rate-x-5m-right-and-rate-sum-x-5m-wro", "sec": "4.1", "q": "Why is <code>sum(rate(x[5m]))<\/code> right and <code>rate(sum(x)[5m])<\/code> wrong?", "a": "<p>Counter resets happen independently per series, and <code>rate()<\/code> only knows how to compensate for resets on an individual series. Summing first mixes resets across pods into garbage (and isn't even valid syntax without a subquery). Always <code>rate()<\/code> first, aggregate second.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#how-do-you-alert-on-the-metric-disappeared-entir", "sec": "4.1", "q": "How do you alert on \"the metric disappeared entirely\"?", "a": "<p>Use <code>absent(up{job=\"x\"} == 1)<\/code> or <code>absent(metric)<\/code>. Standard comparisons return empty when there's no data to compare, so they can never fire; <code>absent()<\/code> explicitly turns \"no series\" into <code>1<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#a-p95-query-returns-nan-two-plausible-reasons", "sec": "4.1", "q": "A p95 query returns NaN. Two plausible reasons?", "a": "<p>Either no requests landed in the lookback window (empty buckets), or your aggregation accidentally dropped the <code>le<\/code> label that <code>histogram_quantile<\/code> needs. Keep it intact with <code>histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le) (rate(x_bucket[5m])))<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#an-exporter-added-a-label-with-one-value-per-req", "sec": "4.1", "q": "An exporter added a label with one value per request. What happens, and what is the fix?", "a": "<p>Instant cardinality explosion: every request creates a brand-new series, blowing up TSDB memory and slowing queries to a crawl. Drop the label or metric immediately via <code>metricRelabelings<\/code>, then strip it from the exporter. High-cardinality IDs belong in logs and traces, never Prometheus labels.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#you-need-to-scrape-a-database-vm-outside-the-clu", "sec": "4.1", "q": "You need to scrape a database VM outside the cluster with the Prometheus Operator, without editing the Prometheus config by hand. Which CRD, and which selector must match?", "a": "<p>Use a <code>ScrapeConfig<\/code> with <code>staticConfigs[].targets: [\"db.example.internal:9187\"]<\/code> (or <code>httpSDConfigs<\/code> / <code>dnsSDConfigs<\/code>), labeled to match Prometheus's <code>scrapeConfigSelector<\/code> and placed in a namespace matched by <code>scrapeConfigNamespaceSelector<\/code> (or Prometheus's own namespace if that selector is nil).<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#predict-that-a-volume-fills-within-a-day-and-ale", "sec": "4.1", "q": "Predict that a volume fills within a day, and alert if any series that should exist has vanished. Two expressions?", "a": "<p>For disk fill: <code>predict_linear(node_filesystem_avail_bytes{mountpoint=\"/data\"}[6h], 24*3600) &lt; 0<\/code>. For a vanished target: <code>absent_over_time(up{job=\"payments\"}[10m])<\/code>, which evaluates to 1 only when zero samples showed up during the entire 10-minute window.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.1#a-servicemonitor-in-monitoring-selects-the-right", "sec": "4.1", "q": "A ServiceMonitor in <code>monitoring<\/code> selects the right labels and port for Services in <code>argocd<\/code>, yet no target appears and Prometheus's own selectors are <code>{}<\/code>. What is missing?", "a": "<p><code>spec.namespaceSelector<\/code> on the ServiceMonitor itself (<code>matchNames: [argocd]<\/code> or <code>any: true<\/code>). Left unset, a ServiceMonitor only looks in its own namespace. Remember the two-hop rule: Prometheus's selector discovers monitors, and the monitor's <code>namespaceSelector<\/code> discovers Services.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#an-alert-shows-firing-in-prometheus-and-nothing-", "sec": "4.2", "q": "An alert shows Firing in Prometheus and nothing arrived. Three candidate causes?", "a": "<p>An active silence matches it, an inhibition rule is suppressing it, or its receiver is failing (check Alertmanager logs and <code>alertmanager_notifications_failed_total<\/code>). And if it only just flipped to Firing, <code>group_wait<\/code> might simply still be ticking down.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#your-rule-applies-cleanly-and-never-evaluates-wh", "sec": "4.2", "q": "Your rule applies cleanly and never evaluates. What did you not check?", "a": "<p>The Prometheus CR's <code>ruleSelector<\/code> and <code>ruleNamespaceSelector<\/code>. Unmatched rules are ignored with zero errors, just like ServiceMonitors - always read the selector first.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#silence-versus-inhibition-when-do-you-use-each", "sec": "4.2", "q": "Silence versus inhibition: when do you use each?", "a": "<p>Use a <strong>silence<\/strong> for temporary, human-driven muting (like a maintenance window) with an expiry and comment. Use <strong>inhibition<\/strong> as a standing architectural rule that suppresses noisy downstream symptoms whenever a bigger upstream alarm is already firing.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#how-long-roughly-from-condition-becomes-true-to-", "sec": "4.2", "q": "How long, roughly, from \"condition becomes true\" to \"notification sent\" on a stock stack?", "a": "<p>Up to one evaluation interval (30s) + your <code>for<\/code> duration (say 1–5m) + <code>group_wait<\/code> (30s) - easily 2–3 minutes at minimum. That's why you wait before declaring alerting broken, and why <code>for: 15m<\/code> is usually too sluggish for page-worthy outages.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#what-is-the-watchdog-alert-for", "sec": "4.2", "q": "What is the Watchdog alert for?", "a": "<p>It fires continuously as a dead-man's switch. An external monitor expects to keep receiving it and pages you if it <em>stops<\/em>, catching the case where Prometheus or Alertmanager itself goes down and can't alert on anything.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#notify-the-database-team-only-during-business-ho", "sec": "4.2", "q": "\"Notify the database team only during business hours, page everyone else at any time.\" Which fields carry that?", "a": "<p>Define a top-level <code>time_intervals<\/code> window (<code>times: 09:00-17:00<\/code>, <code>weekdays: monday:friday<\/code>, plus <code>location<\/code>) and attach <code>active_time_intervals: [business_hours]<\/code> to the database route so notifications pause outside those hours while other routes fire 24/7. (<code>mute_time_intervals<\/code> is the inverse, and mute wins if both appear.)<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#a-team-s-alertmanagerconfig-in-namespace-team-a-", "sec": "4.2", "q": "A team's <code>AlertmanagerConfig<\/code> in namespace <code>team-a<\/code> routes <code>alertname=\"NodeDown\"<\/code> to their webhook, is selected, appears in the rendered config, and never fires. Why?", "a": "<p>The operator automatically scopes merged namespaced <code>AlertmanagerConfig<\/code> routes with <code>namespace=\"team-a\"<\/code>. Because <code>NodeDown<\/code> is a cluster-level alert carrying no <code>namespace<\/code> label, the route can never match it. Route cluster-wide alerts through global config (<code>spec.configSecret<\/code> or <code>spec.alertmanagerConfiguration<\/code>).<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#explain-the-14-4-in-a-burn-rate-alert-and-why-th", "sec": "4.2", "q": "Explain the 14.4 in a burn-rate alert and why the rule has two windows.", "a": "<p>A burn rate of 14.4 consumes 2% of a 30-day error budget in one hour (30 × 24 × 0.02 = 14.4). Pairing a long window (1h) with a short window (5m) via <code>and<\/code> confirms the burn is sustained while letting the alert clear quickly as soon as the issue recovers.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.2#how-do-you-prove-a-new-alerting-rule-fires-under", "sec": "4.2", "q": "How do you prove a new alerting rule fires under the right conditions without waiting for production to break?", "a": "<p>Run <code>promtool test rules<\/code> with synthetic <code>input_series<\/code> and <code>alert_rule_test<\/code> assertions checking expected labels and annotations at specific timestamps. Pair it with <code>promtool check rules<\/code> for syntax and <code>amtool config routes test<\/code> to verify routing.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#how-does-a-dashboard-get-into-grafana-in-a-gitop", "sec": "4.3", "q": "How does a dashboard get into Grafana in a GitOps platform, and why not click it?", "a": "<p>Ship the dashboard JSON inside a ConfigMap labeled <code>grafana_dashboard: \"1\"<\/code> from Git, and let Grafana's sidecar load it. Dashboards clicked together in the UI live only inside Grafana's database - unreviewed, unversioned, and wiped out on pod restarts without persistent storage.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#explain-red-and-use-and-which-you-would-use-for-", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Explain RED and USE, and which you would use for a queue worker.", "a": "<p><strong>RED<\/strong> (Rate, Errors, Duration) fits request-driven services; <strong>USE<\/strong> (Utilization, Saturation, Errors) fits hardware and pools. A queue worker benefits from both plus queue depth: USE for worker pool capacity, RED-like metrics for message processing, and backlog growth as your saturation signal.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#loki-returns-nothing-for-namespace-team-a-two-ho", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Loki returns nothing for <code>{namespace=\"team-a\"}<\/code>. Two hops to check, in order.", "a": "<p>Check the shipper first (is Alloy running, tailing that namespace, and pushing without errors?), then Loki (is the ingester healthy, is retention dropping the window, and are multi-tenant org headers right?). A healthy Loki with a broken shipper stays quietly empty.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#why-is-a-per-request-id-label-a-bad-idea-in-loki", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Why is a per-request-ID label a bad idea in Loki?", "a": "<p>Every unique label set spawns a new stream and index entry. A per-request label creates millions of tiny streams, thrashing memory and slowing queries. Keep high-cardinality IDs in the log body (or structured metadata) and filter them at query time.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#turn-alert-me-when-payments-start-failing-into-a", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Turn \"alert me when payments start failing\" into a LogQL-based rule, conceptually.", "a": "<p>Write a LogQL metric query such as <code>sum(rate({namespace=\"payments\"} |= \"payment_failed\" [5m])) &gt; 0.1<\/code> and evaluate it in Loki's ruler (or remote-write it into Prometheus via a recording rule). From there, Alertmanager routes it just like any Prometheus alert.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#a-dashboard-imported-from-a-configmap-shows-data", "sec": "4.3", "q": "A dashboard imported from a ConfigMap shows \"datasource not found\" on every panel. What did the export get wrong, and what is the durable fix?", "a": "<p>It was exported with \"export for sharing externally\" enabled (turning datasources into unbound <code>${DS_PROMETHEUS}<\/code> variables) or references a random UID that doesn't exist in this cluster. Fix it by provisioning datasources with explicit stable <code>uid<\/code>s and exporting with external sharing off - or mapping <code>datasources[]<\/code> in a Grafana Operator <code>GrafanaDashboard<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#why-is-a-trace-id-a-bad-loki-label-but-a-good-ca", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Why is a trace ID a bad Loki label but a good candidate for structured metadata, and how do you still search it quickly?", "a": "<p>As an index label, every unique trace ID mints a separate stream and blows up Loki's index. As structured metadata, key-value pairs ride inside the chunks without spawning streams while staying fast to filter using regular label syntax (<code>| trace_id=\"abc\"<\/code>). Alloy and OTLP ingest route high-cardinality attributes there automatically.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.3#your-shipper-is-a-promtail-daemonset-from-an-old", "sec": "4.3", "q": "Your shipper is a Promtail DaemonSet from an older tutorial. Why is that a problem in 2026, and what replaces it?", "a": "<p>Promtail hit end-of-life on March 2, 2026 with no further security or bug fixes. Migrate to Grafana Alloy (<code>alloy convert --source-format=promtail<\/code> handles the config translation), or use vendor-neutral shippers like the OpenTelemetry Collector (<code>filelog<\/code> receiver) or Fluent Bit.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#jaeger-shows-hundreds-of-one-span-traces-diagnos", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Jaeger shows hundreds of one-span traces. Diagnosis?", "a": "<p>Context propagation is broken, so every service starts its own orphan trace. Check for a proxy stripping <code>traceparent<\/code>, a propagator mismatch (W3C vs. B3), a hand-rolled HTTP client skipping header injection, or an uninstrumented async queue hop.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#head-based-versus-tail-based-sampling-when-does-", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Head-based versus tail-based sampling: when does the difference bite?", "a": "<p>Whenever rare failures matter most. Head sampling at 1% usually discards the exact 5-second timeout or 500 error you needed to debug. Tail sampling inspects finished traces so you keep every error and slow request, trading extra collector memory and trace-ID-aware routing across replicas.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#where-should-sampling-and-redaction-be-configure", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Where should sampling and redaction be configured, and why there?", "a": "<p>In the OpenTelemetry Collector. It's the platform's central control plane: one config edit updates sampling and PII redaction across the entire fleet without begging forty app teams to redeploy their SDKs.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#you-annotated-a-deployment-for-auto-instrumentat", "sec": "4.4", "q": "You annotated a Deployment for auto-instrumentation and nothing changed. Two most likely reasons?", "a": "<p>Either the annotation went on the Deployment's metadata instead of <code>spec.template.metadata<\/code>, or you haven't restarted the pods yet (webhook injection only fires at pod creation). Third check: verify the <code>&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;<\/code> value matches a real <code>Instrumentation<\/code> CR.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#how-do-traces-metrics-and-logs-tie-together-in-p", "sec": "4.4", "q": "How do traces, metrics and logs tie together in practice?", "a": "<p>Exemplars link histogram latency spikes straight to sample trace IDs, structured logs include the trace ID so you can pivot directly from a slow span into its exact log lines, and the Collector stamps shared Kubernetes resource attributes across all three signals.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#your-tail-sampling-collector-runs-three-replicas", "sec": "4.4", "q": "Your tail-sampling collector runs three replicas behind a Service, and the sampling decisions look random. Why, and what fixes it?", "a": "<p>A normal Kubernetes Service spreads spans from the same trace across different replicas, so no collector ever sees the full trace before <code>decision_wait<\/code> fires. Put a front-tier collector with a <code>loadbalancing<\/code> exporter (<code>routing_key: traceID<\/code>) ahead of a StatefulSet sampling tier - or run a single sampling replica.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#what-is-jaeger-v2-architecturally-and-where-does", "sec": "4.4", "q": "What is Jaeger v2 architecturally, and where does the old jaeger-agent go?", "a": "<p>Jaeger v2 is a distribution of the OpenTelemetry Collector: one binary configured in Collector YAML, running as <code>collector<\/code>, <code>query<\/code>, <code>ingester<\/code>, or <code>all-in-one<\/code> with Jaeger extensions for storage, query UI (16686), and remote sampling, ingesting standard OTLP on 4317/4318. The legacy <code>jaeger-agent<\/code> is gone - run a standard OpenTelemetry Collector as a DaemonSet or sidecar instead.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#a-pod-has-the-injected-init-container-and-the-ri", "sec": "4.4", "q": "A pod has the injected init container and the right annotation, yet no spans appear. Two collector-side reasons and one network reason?", "a": "<p>Collector/config: the <code>Instrumentation<\/code> endpoint uses the wrong port for the language agent's protocol (Python only speaks HTTP on 4318, while Java defaults to gRPC on 4317), or the Collector has no <code>traces<\/code> pipeline wired to that receiver. Network: a default-deny NetworkPolicy blocks egress to the collector namespace. Inspect <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT<\/code> inside the pod and test the port directly.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.4#how-does-a-metric-panel-jump-to-a-trace-and-what", "sec": "4.4", "q": "How does a metric panel jump to a trace, and what has to be enabled at each layer?", "a": "<p>Through exemplars: the app or <code>span_metrics<\/code> connector attaches <code>trace_id<\/code> to histogram buckets, Prometheus retains them when <code>--enable-feature=exemplar-storage<\/code> is enabled, and Grafana maps <code>trace_id<\/code> to Jaeger or Tempo via <code>exemplarTraceIdDestinations<\/code>. Logs join the same loop through Loki's <code>derivedFields<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#name-the-dora-four-and-the-system-that-holds-eac", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Name the DORA four and the system that holds each one's raw data in this lab.", "a": "<p>Deployment frequency: Argo CD sync counters. Lead time: Git commit timestamps joined with CD deploy timestamps. Change failure rate: sync phases and rollout outcomes. Time to restore: alert history (firing to resolved) or incident records. None of them live in one single tool, which is why wiring telemetry together matters.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#why-increase-rather-than-the-raw-counter-for-dep", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Why <code>increase()<\/code> rather than the raw counter for deployment frequency?", "a": "<p>A raw counter climbs forever from startup and resets whenever the pod restarts. <code>increase(...[24h])<\/code> gives you the exact count inside your window while handling resets cleanly - far more readable than a tiny per-second <code>rate()<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#your-new-servicemonitor-for-argo-cd-produces-no-", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Your new ServiceMonitor for Argo CD produces no target. First two checks?", "a": "<p>Verify <code>endpoints[].port<\/code> matches the Service's exact port <em>name<\/em> (<code>http-metrics<\/code>, not <code>metrics<\/code> or <code>8082<\/code>) and confirm your label selector matches the Service's real labels. Then check Prometheus's <code>serviceMonitorSelector<\/code> and the monitor's <code>namespaceSelector<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#how-would-you-measure-request-to-fulfillment-for", "sec": "4.5", "q": "How would you measure \"request to fulfillment\" for a self-service API?", "a": "<p>Measure the delta between a custom resource's <code>metadata.creationTimestamp<\/code> and the <code>lastTransitionTime<\/code> of its <code>Ready<\/code> condition, export it as a metric (via kube-state-metrics <code>CustomResourceStateMetrics<\/code> or a small exporter), and track p50/p95 over time.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#someone-proposes-measuring-developer-productivit", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Someone proposes measuring developer productivity by lines of code. Reply in one sentence.", "a": "<p>Measure outcomes, not raw volume: DORA delivery metrics paired with platform-effectiveness measures (fulfillment latency, adoption, developer satisfaction) capture real value delivery, whereas line counts are trivially gamed and reward bloat.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#name-the-five-dora-metrics-as-the-2024-report-de", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Name the five DORA metrics as the 2024 report defines them, and say which group each belongs to.", "a": "<p>Throughput: change lead time, deployment frequency, and failed deployment recovery time. Stability: change failure rate and deployment rework rate (unplanned hotfixes for user-facing bugs, added in 2024). Operational reliability is tracked alongside them rather than as a sixth key, and the 2025 report moved from four clusters to team archetypes.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#the-white-paper-s-organizational-efficiency-grou", "sec": "4.5", "q": "The white paper's \"organizational efficiency\" group: which three latencies does it name, and how would you get the first one out of this lab?", "a": "<p>Request-to-fulfillment latency for a capability, time to build and deploy a brand-new service to production, and time for a new developer to submit their first change. Pull the first from <code>metadata.creationTimestamp<\/code> to the <code>Ready<\/code> condition's <code>lastTransitionTime<\/code> on our Crossplane XR or kro instance, exposed via <code>CustomResourceStateMetrics<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.5#which-platform-tool-metric-would-you-alert-on-to", "sec": "4.5", "q": "Which platform-tool metric would you alert on to learn that the platform, not the workloads, is degrading, and what kind of SLO fits it?", "a": "<p>Alert on control-plane SLIs: Argo CD sync success ratio (<code>argocd_app_sync_total<\/code>), Crossplane/kro reconcile error rate (<code>controller_runtime_reconcile_total{result=\"error\"}<\/code>), or webhook admission latency. Give it a 30-day availability SLO (like 99.5% clean reconciles) guarded by section 4.2's multi-window burn-rate rules.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#every-pod-in-a-namespace-is-running-and-the-app-", "sec": "4.6", "q": "Every pod in a namespace is Running and the app returns nothing. First three moves?", "a": "<p>Force a real request (run <code>curl<\/code> or <code>nslookup<\/code> from inside the namespace) so you have live evidence; inspect the Service's EndpointSlice and pod readiness; then check Hubble for DROPPED NetworkPolicy verdicts and DNS resolution. \"Running\" only proves kubelet started the container.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#a-pod-is-crashloopbackoff-and-kubectl-logs-shows", "sec": "4.6", "q": "A pod is CrashLoopBackOff and <code>kubectl logs<\/code> shows nothing useful.", "a": "<p>Run <code>kubectl logs --previous<\/code> so you read the container that actually crashed rather than the fresh one still starting. Follow up with <code>kubectl describe pod<\/code> and <code>lastState.terminated<\/code> for the exit code and reason (137 + OOMKilled points to memory; 1 with stack trace points to app config).<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#nothing-can-be-created-cluster-wide-every-apply-", "sec": "4.6", "q": "Nothing can be created cluster-wide; every apply times out with a webhook error. What happened and what is the lever?", "a": "<p>An admission webhook's backend is down while its <code>failurePolicy<\/code> is set to <code>Fail<\/code>, blocking every matching write. Fix the backend Deployment first - or, in a genuine emergency, scope or remove the webhook configuration temporarily (section 5.2).<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#you-have-four-minutes-left-and-no-theory-that-fi", "sec": "4.6", "q": "You have four minutes left and no theory that fits the evidence. What do you do?", "a": "<p>Pull back to Step 1 and widen: re-check blast radius and recent events instead of digging deeper into the wrong object. Under exam pressure, flag the task and move on - getting stuck on one hard task costs you two easy ones.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#what-proves-an-incident-is-over", "sec": "4.6", "q": "What proves an incident is over?", "a": "<p>Re-running the exact check that failed and seeing it pass: the broken <code>curl<\/code> returns 200, the alert clears, or the validation probe succeeds. A pod saying Running never proves recovery on its own.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#every-write-to-the-cluster-fails-with-etcdserver", "sec": "4.6", "q": "Every write to the cluster fails with <code>etcdserver: mvcc: database space exceeded<\/code> but reads work. What happened and what is the order of operations?", "a": "<p>etcd filled its backend quota (2 GiB default) and tripped the <code>NOSPACE<\/code> alarm, flipping the store read-only. Run compact → defragment every member → <code>etcdctl alarm disarm<\/code> (in that exact order), raise <code>--quota-backend-bytes<\/code> if growth is legitimate, and track down whatever flooded etcd (event storms, giant ConfigMaps, or a looping controller).<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#a-container-exits-with-code-143-every-30-seconds", "sec": "4.6", "q": "A container exits with code 143 every 30 seconds and the app logs look healthy. Diagnosis?", "a": "<p>Exit code 143 is SIGTERM - Kubernetes is gracefully killing it on a timer, which almost always means a failing liveness probe (wrong port/path, or timeout too short for startup). Check <code>kubectl describe pod<\/code> for <code>Liveness probe failed<\/code> events and fix the probe or add a <code>startupProbe<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#you-roll-a-deployment-back-with-kubectl-rollout-", "sec": "4.6", "q": "You roll a Deployment back with <code>kubectl rollout undo<\/code> and two minutes later it is broken again. Why, and what should you have done?", "a": "<p>Your GitOps controller reconciled Git back onto the cluster. Either pause reconciliation first (<code>argocd app set x --sync-policy none<\/code> or <code>flux suspend kustomization x<\/code>) for an emergency undo, or go straight to reverting the Git commit so the controller rolls back cleanly for you.<\/p>"},
{"id": "4.6#which-kubectl-debug-invocation-gives-you-tcpdump", "sec": "4.6", "q": "Which <code>kubectl debug<\/code> invocation gives you tcpdump on a node without full root, and why does the default node debug pod fail at <code>chroot /host<\/code>?", "a": "<p>Run <code>kubectl debug node/&lt;n&gt; -it --image=&lt;image with tcpdump&gt; --profile=netadmin<\/code> to grant <code>NET_ADMIN<\/code> and <code>NET_RAW<\/code> without full privilege. The default <code>general<\/code> profile mounts <code>/host<\/code> unprivileged, so host-root operations like <code>chroot<\/code> fail unless you pass <code>--profile=sysadmin<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#define-developer-once-and-grant-it-in-twelve-nam", "sec": "5.1", "q": "Define \"developer\" once and grant it in twelve namespaces. What objects, and how many?", "a": "<p>One ClusterRole plus twelve RoleBindings (one per namespace, each pointing its <code>roleRef<\/code> at that ClusterRole). Never a ClusterRoleBinding: that would grant permissions cluster-wide and fail any \"not in another namespace\" check.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#a-user-can-get-a-pod-by-name-but-kubectl-get-pod", "sec": "5.1", "q": "A user can <code>get<\/code> a pod by name but <code>kubectl get pods<\/code> fails. Why?", "a": "<p><code>list<\/code> is a separate verb from <code>get<\/code>, and a rule scoped with <code>resourceNames<\/code> can't satisfy an unfiltered <code>list<\/code>. Add <code>list<\/code> (and <code>watch<\/code> if anything streams updates), knowing that <code>list<\/code> exposes every object of that kind in the namespace.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#how-do-you-let-a-tenant-read-pod-logs-but-never-", "sec": "5.1", "q": "How do you let a tenant read pod logs but never exec into a pod?", "a": "<p>Grant <code>get<\/code> on <code>pods/log<\/code> and withhold <code>create<\/code> on <code>pods/exec<\/code>. Because Kubernetes treats subresources as distinct resource strings, you can separate them cleanly.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#you-ship-a-new-crd-how-do-existing-view-users-ge", "sec": "5.1", "q": "You ship a new CRD. How do existing <code>view<\/code> users get read access without editing the built-in role?", "a": "<p>Create a ClusterRole granting read verbs on your new kind and label it <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/aggregate-to-view: \"true\"<\/code>. Role aggregation folds it straight into the built-in <code>view<\/code> role automatically, which is the standard platform move when shipping a CRD.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#sealed-secrets-versus-eso-which-would-you-pick-f", "sec": "5.1", "q": "Sealed Secrets versus ESO: which would you pick for a regulated environment with an existing vault, and why?", "a": "<p>Pick ESO. Your vault remains the single source of truth with its own audit trail and rotation policies, and git only ever stores references rather than secret material (even encrypted). Sealed Secrets fits self-contained clusters without an external store, at the cost of managing and backing up the sealing keys yourself.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#is-a-kubernetes-secret-encrypted", "sec": "5.1", "q": "Is a Kubernetes Secret encrypted?", "a": "<p>Not by default: base64 is just encoding. At rest in etcd, Secrets are only encrypted if the API server has an <code>EncryptionConfiguration<\/code> (optionally backed by a KMS); in transit, TLS protects them. In practice, strict RBAC over who can read Secrets does most of the day-to-day protection.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#a-namespace-admin-with-the-built-in-admin-role-c", "sec": "5.1", "q": "A namespace admin with the built-in <code>admin<\/code> role cannot create a RoleBinding to a custom ClusterRole that grants <code>nodes<\/code> read. Why, and what is the least-privilege fix?", "a": "<p>Escalation prevention: you can only bind permissions you already hold, and <code>admin<\/code> doesn't include cluster-scoped <code>nodes<\/code>. The least-privilege fix is granting the <code>bind<\/code> verb on <code>clusterroles<\/code> scoped via <code>resourceNames<\/code> to that exact ClusterRole. Handing out node read access directly or granting <code>cluster-admin<\/code> would work, and would fail least privilege.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#an-oidc-user-in-group-platform-team-is-bound-via", "sec": "5.1", "q": "An OIDC user in group <code>platform-team<\/code> is bound via a ClusterRoleBinding to that group and still gets Forbidden. First command, likely cause?", "a": "<p>Run <code>kubectl auth whoami<\/code> to see the exact groups the API server receives. With <code>--oidc-groups-prefix<\/code> (or <code>claimMappings.groups.prefix<\/code> in structured config), the group arrives prefixed, like <code>oidc:platform-team<\/code>, so bind to that exact string. The same check also catches username prefixes and mismatched groups claim names.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#you-rotate-the-aescbc-key-in-encryptionconfigura", "sec": "5.1", "q": "You rotate the aescbc key in <code>EncryptionConfiguration<\/code>. Order of operations, and what happens if you simply replace the key?", "a": "<p>Add the new key first in <code>providers[]<\/code> while keeping the old key second, restart the API servers, rewrite every Secret so it re-encrypts under the new key (<code>kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -<\/code>), and finally remove the old key. If you simply swap the key out, every existing Secret becomes unreadable, failing loudly on <code>get secrets<\/code> rather than at API server startup.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.1#eso-a-database-password-rotates-in-vault-every-h", "sec": "5.1", "q": "ESO: a database password rotates in Vault every hour, the ExternalSecret has <code>refreshInterval: 0<\/code>, and pods keep failing to log in. Two fixes and their trade-offs?", "a": "<p><code>refreshInterval: 0<\/code> means \"create once and never refresh\", so the Secret stays frozen on the original password. Option one: set a <code>refreshInterval<\/code> shorter than the rotation window (with <code>Periodic<\/code> policy) and ensure pods pick up updates (via file mounts or a restart trigger like Reloader, since env vars don't reload). Option two: if running pods shouldn't see credentials shift underneath them, keep <code>CreatedOnce<\/code> with <code>immutable: true<\/code> and roll deployments intentionally on rotation.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#a-require-requests-policy-never-fires-in-a-tenan", "sec": "5.2", "q": "A \"require requests\" policy never fires in a tenant namespace. Why?", "a": "<p>The namespace's <code>LimitRange<\/code> injects default requests during <em>mutating<\/em> admission, before validating webhooks execute, so the pod Kyverno sees already complies. Admission ordering (mutate first, then validate) explains this completely, and it applies to every defaulting mechanism.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#failurepolicy-fail-versus-ignore-argue-both-then", "sec": "5.2", "q": "<code>failurePolicy: Fail<\/code> versus <code>Ignore<\/code>: argue both, then say what bounds the risk.", "a": "<p><code>Fail<\/code> prevents unvalidated writes from sneaking past, at the cost of blocking cluster writes if the webhook goes down (a policy outage becomes a cluster outage). <code>Ignore<\/code> keeps deployments moving during an outage, at the cost of a policy bypass window. Bound the blast radius with a tight <code>namespaceSelector<\/code>, low <code>timeoutSeconds<\/code>, system-namespace exemptions, and enough controller replicas that the webhook isn't a single point of failure.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#you-tighten-a-policy-to-deny-what-happens-to-the", "sec": "5.2", "q": "You tighten a policy to Deny. What happens to the workloads that already violate it?", "a": "<p>Nothing right away, because admission only checks writes. Existing pods keep running until their next write (a rollout, scale event, or node eviction), and then fail unexpectedly, often at 3 a.m. That's why you follow Audit → report → fix → Enforce and clear the reports before flipping the dial.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#which-engine-would-you-pick-to-also-create-a-def", "sec": "5.2", "q": "Which engine would you pick to also <em>create<\/em> a default NetworkPolicy in every new namespace?", "a": "<p>Kyverno. Resource generation is first-class there: a <code>GeneratingPolicy<\/code> (or a classic <code>generate<\/code> rule on older clusters) creates downstream objects and keeps them synced. Gatekeeper validates and mutates but doesn't generate, and ValidatingAdmissionPolicy only validates. Match the engine's capabilities to the requirement.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#why-might-a-platform-team-prefer-validatingadmis", "sec": "5.2", "q": "Why might a platform team prefer ValidatingAdmissionPolicy for a simple rule?", "a": "<p>Zero extra controllers, zero webhooks, zero availability dependencies, and no version skew: the API server runs CEL right in-process. The trade-off is a narrower feature set (no generation, no built-in reports, and no mutation on older Kubernetes releases), making it ideal for crisp invariants rather than a full governance suite.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#a-kyverno-validatingpolicy-denies-bare-pods-but-", "sec": "5.2", "q": "A Kyverno ValidatingPolicy denies bare pods but a Deployment with the same violating template is admitted. Field?", "a": "<p>Set <code>spec.autogen.podControllers.controllers<\/code> to include <code>deployments<\/code> (along with <code>statefulsets<\/code>, <code>daemonsets<\/code>, <code>jobs<\/code>, and <code>cronjobs<\/code> as needed). Autogen rewrites your Pod rule to validate controller <code>spec.template<\/code> blocks directly. Without it, the Deployment gets admitted and its pods fail silently one layer down on the ReplicaSet (the same indirection as PSS). Remember that pod-controller autogen and <code>autogen.validatingAdmissionPolicy<\/code> are mutually exclusive.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#which-kyverno-controller-flag-must-be-on-before-", "sec": "5.2", "q": "Which Kyverno controller flag must be on before a <code>PolicyException<\/code> has any effect, and how does the report show an applied exception?", "a": "<p>Only <code>--enablePolicyException=true<\/code> is mandatory. The companion flag, <code>--exceptionNamespace=&lt;namespace&gt;<\/code>, is optional: when set, only exceptions inside that namespace take effect; when omitted (as in this lab), exceptions work from any namespace while emitting a warning (\"The exceptionNamespace flag is not set\") on creation. When an exception matches, the <code>PolicyReport<\/code> logs the rule as <code>skip<\/code> with the exception's name in its result properties across both admission and background scans.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#a-gatekeeper-constraint-is-applied-the-violating", "sec": "5.2", "q": "A Gatekeeper Constraint is applied, the violating object is still admitted, and <code>status.totalViolations<\/code> grows. Two likely settings?", "a": "<p>Either the Constraint has <code>spec.enforcementAction: dryrun<\/code> (or <code>warn<\/code>), or the Gatekeeper webhook is running with <code>failurePolicy: Ignore<\/code> (its install default) while the webhook pod is unhealthy or timing out. In both cases, the audit loop still scans and bumps the violation count even though admission never blocks. Switch the Constraint to <code>deny<\/code> and inspect the webhook's <code>failurePolicy<\/code> and <code>timeoutSeconds<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.2#you-write-a-validatingadmissionpolicy-with-a-mis", "sec": "5.2", "q": "You write a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy with a misspelt field path. Is it rejected? Where do you find out, and what decides the runtime effect?", "a": "<p>No, Kubernetes accepts it. CEL type errors show up afterward in <code>status.typeChecking<\/code> rather than as an apply error. At runtime, evaluating the bad field path throws an error, and <code>failurePolicy<\/code> decides what happens next: <code>Fail<\/code> blocks every matching write (per the binding's <code>validationActions<\/code>), while <code>Ignore<\/code> lets them slip through. Inspect <code>status.typeChecking<\/code>, then fix the path.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#name-the-three-profiles-in-one-clause-each", "sec": "5.3", "q": "Name the three profiles in one clause each.", "a": "<p><strong>Privileged:<\/strong> completely unrestricted. <strong>Baseline:<\/strong> blocks known privilege escalations (privileged containers, host namespaces, <code>hostPath<\/code>, unsafe capabilities). <strong>Restricted:<\/strong> actively requires non-root, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false<\/code>, all capabilities dropped, and <code>RuntimeDefault<\/code> (or <code>Localhost<\/code>) seccomp.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#a-deployment-is-created-and-no-pods-appear-where", "sec": "5.3", "q": "A Deployment is created and no pods appear. Where is the PSS error?", "a": "<p>On the ReplicaSet. Because PSS enforces at the pod level, the Deployment succeeds and the ReplicaSet controller logs the rejection one level down. Check <code>kubectl describe rs -l …<\/code> or namespace events.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#you-want-to-know-who-breaks-if-you-tighten-a-nam", "sec": "5.3", "q": "You want to know who breaks if you tighten a namespace. Command?", "a": "<p><code>kubectl label --dry-run=server --overwrite ns &lt;ns&gt; pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted<\/code>. The API server evaluates every running pod against the proposed label and prints a warning for each offender without modifying anything.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#why-pin-enforce-version", "sec": "5.3", "q": "Why pin <code>enforce-version<\/code>?", "a": "<p>Because PSS profiles gain stricter checks across Kubernetes releases. Leaving it on <code>latest<\/code> means a routine cluster upgrade can start rejecting pods that deployed cleanly before. Pinning turns that into a scheduled migration instead of a surprise outage.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#pss-or-kyverno-for-images-must-come-from-our-reg", "sec": "5.3", "q": "PSS or Kyverno for \"images must come from our registry\"?", "a": "<p>Kyverno (or Gatekeeper). PSS only covers the fixed pod-hardening profile and knows nothing about image registries. Treat PSS as the always-on floor and run a policy engine alongside it for organization-specific rules.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#a-pod-passes-restricted-admission-and-then-sits-", "sec": "5.3", "q": "A pod passes restricted admission and then sits in <code>CreateContainerConfigError<\/code> with <code>container has runAsNonRoot and image will run as root<\/code>. What happened?", "a": "<p>Setting <code>runAsNonRoot: true<\/code> satisfied PSS at admission, but the container image's default user is root, so the kubelet refused to launch it. Set <code>runAsUser<\/code> (and <code>runAsGroup<\/code>) to a non-zero UID, or switch to an image built with a non-root <code>USER<\/code>. Remember: PSS checks the pod spec; the kubelet checks the image.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#how-do-you-make-every-namespace-without-labels-e", "sec": "5.3", "q": "How do you make every namespace without labels enforce baseline and warn on restricted, cluster-wide, and what does a namespace label do to that default?", "a": "<p>Configure an <code>AdmissionConfiguration<\/code> containing a <code>PodSecurityConfiguration<\/code> whose <code>defaults<\/code> set <code>enforce: baseline<\/code> and <code>warn: restricted<\/code> (with versions), and pass it to <code>kube-apiserver<\/code> via <code>--admission-control-config-file<\/code>. Any namespace label overrides the default for that mode only. On a managed cluster where you can't touch API server flags, use a policy engine to stamp those labels onto namespaces as they're created.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.3#restricted-rejects-a-container-for-seccompprofil", "sec": "5.3", "q": "Restricted rejects a container for <code>seccompProfile<\/code>, yet the kubelet runs with <code>seccompDefault: true<\/code>. Why is that not enough?", "a": "<p>PSS inspects the pod spec at admission, whereas the kubelet default only takes effect later at container creation and is invisible to admission control. Restricted requires <code>seccompProfile.type<\/code> to be explicitly set to <code>RuntimeDefault<\/code> or <code>Localhost<\/code> in the spec (at pod level or on every container). The kubelet flag is still worth enabling to cover pods in non-restricted namespaces.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#which-audit-level-would-you-set-for-secrets-and-", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Which audit level would you set for Secrets, and which for Events? Why?", "a": "<p>Secrets go at <code>Metadata<\/code>, never <code>Request<\/code> or <code>RequestResponse<\/code>, so you don't leak secret values straight into log files. Events go at <code>None<\/code> because they're noisy, high-volume, and low-value. Because rules are first-match-wins, put both near the top of your policy file.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#find-every-request-a-specific-serviceaccount-was", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Find every request a specific ServiceAccount was denied. What does the query look like?", "a": "<p>Filter on <code>.user.username == \"system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;name&gt;\"<\/code> paired with either <code>.responseStatus.code == 403<\/code> or <code>.annotations[\"authorization.k8s.io/decision\"] == \"forbid\"<\/code>, then project out <code>verb<\/code> and <code>objectRef<\/code>. That's the audit-log twin of the RBAC debugging you practiced in 5.1.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#an-sbom-and-a-vulnerability-report-what-is-the-d", "sec": "5.4", "q": "An SBOM and a vulnerability report: what is the difference and why keep both?", "a": "<p>An SBOM is a static inventory of components and versions; a vulnerability report is that inventory matched against a CVE database at one moment in time. Keep both because the inventory stays constant while new CVEs appear every day, which is why continuous rescanning via an operator beats scanning only at build time.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#why-is-compliance-as-crds-a-platform-engineering", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Why is \"compliance as CRDs\" a platform-engineering idea rather than a Trivy feature?", "a": "<p>Because storing compliance findings as Kubernetes API objects makes them queryable with kubectl, governed by RBAC, watchable by controllers, graphable in Grafana, and reviewable in git. Static reports dumped into an object bucket can't integrate cleanly with anything else your platform already runs.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#your-cluster-s-api-server-will-not-start-after-a", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Your cluster's API server will not start after an audit change. First hypothesis?", "a": "<p>The policy file at <code>--audit-policy-file<\/code> is missing, unreadable, or has invalid YAML (or its <code>hostPath<\/code> mount in the static pod manifest is broken). By design, <code>kube-apiserver<\/code> refuses to start rather than run silently unaudited, so a small audit mistake looks like a dead control plane.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#your-audit-policy-puts-the-catch-all-metadata-ru", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Your audit policy puts the catch-all <code>Metadata<\/code> rule first and the Secrets rule after it. What is wrong, and what else should precede the catch-all?", "a": "<p>First match wins, so putting the catch-all first makes every rule below it dead code. Here Secrets still land at <code>Metadata<\/code> by luck, but any <code>None<\/code> rule below the catch-all stops filtering noise, and any <code>RequestResponse<\/code> rule below it stops capturing RBAC diffs. Always order from top to bottom: <code>None<\/code> for health URLs, leases, and kube-proxy watches; <code>Metadata<\/code> for secrets, configmaps, tokenreviews, and <code>pods/exec<\/code>; <code>RequestResponse<\/code> for RBAC and policy groups; and the catch-all last.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#which-layer-catches-each-a-user-reads-a-secret-a", "sec": "5.4", "q": "Which layer catches each: a user reads a Secret; a process in a container starts <code>/bin/sh<\/code>; an image contains a package with a new CVE; a node's kubelet allows anonymous auth?", "a": "<p>In order: the API audit log (<code>Metadata<\/code> rule on <code>secrets<\/code>), Falco syscall rules, the Trivy Operator's <code>VulnerabilityReport<\/code> on rescan (or a pipeline scan at build time), and <code>kube-bench<\/code> or the Trivy Operator's <code>InfraAssessmentReport<\/code> / CIS compliance report. Four distinct sensors telling one compliance story; naming which layer catches what is how you nail \"design a complete audit trail\".<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.4#what-is-the-difference-between-an-sbom-attestati", "sec": "5.4", "q": "What is the difference between an SBOM attestation and a VEX document, and why keep both?", "a": "<p>The SBOM lists every component inside an artifact, while VEX states whether specific CVEs in those components actually affect the product (not affected, affected, fixed, or under investigation) along with a justification. Scanners match the SBOM against CVE feeds and then apply VEX to suppress non-applicable findings with a documented rationale, which is auditable in a way a bare <code>.trivyignore<\/code> entry isn't.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#we-installed-a-service-mesh-so-all-traffic-is-en", "sec": "5.5", "q": "\"We installed a service mesh, so all traffic is encrypted.\" Correct?", "a": "<p>Not yet. By default, Istio runs in <code>PERMISSIVE<\/code> mode so teams can migrate gradually, meaning plaintext still gets accepted alongside mTLS. You need a <code>PeerAuthentication<\/code> set to <code>STRICT<\/code> (mesh-wide, per namespace, or per workload), and you prove it by watching an unmeshed plaintext client get rejected.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#where-does-a-workload-s-mesh-identity-come-from-", "sec": "5.5", "q": "Where does a workload's mesh identity come from, and why does that matter to section 5.1?", "a": "<p>Straight from its ServiceAccount: <code>spiffe://cluster.local/ns/&lt;ns&gt;/sa/&lt;sa&gt;<\/code>. That means your RBAC design from 5.1 now doubles as your network authorization boundary (one identity, two enforcement points), and letting unrelated apps share the <code>default<\/code> SA quietly weakens both.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#you-need-to-allow-only-post-to-orders-from-one-s", "sec": "5.5", "q": "You need to allow only POST to <code>/orders<\/code> from one service. What must exist?", "a": "<p>An <code>AuthorizationPolicy<\/code> with an L7 <code>to.operation<\/code> rule <em>and<\/em> a waypoint proxy enrolled for the target. ztunnel only speaks L4, so without a waypoint nothing can inspect HTTP methods or paths.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#networkpolicy-or-authorizationpolicy-when-do-you", "sec": "5.5", "q": "NetworkPolicy or AuthorizationPolicy: when do you use each?", "a": "<p>Use both as complementary layers. <code>NetworkPolicy<\/code> runs in the CNI using pod labels and covers all traffic, including unmeshed workloads. <code>AuthorizationPolicy<\/code> runs in the mesh using cryptographic certificates, surviving IP churn and crossing cluster boundaries. Together they give you defense in depth: the CNI shrinks the blast radius while the mesh verifies who's actually calling.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#give-a-scenario-where-spire-beats-a-mesh", "sec": "5.5", "q": "Give a scenario where SPIRE beats a mesh.", "a": "<p>A pod needs to authenticate to something outside the mesh (a managed database, a partner API, or a legacy VM service) with short-lived credentials and no static secrets. SPIRE delivers an SVID over the local Workload API socket, whereas a mesh only governs traffic between meshed workloads. Bonus: SPIFFE trust bundles federate cleanly across trust domains, extending identity beyond a single cluster.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#you-add-to-operation-paths-to-an-existing-ambien", "sec": "5.5", "q": "You add <code>to.operation.paths<\/code> to an existing ambient AuthorizationPolicy that uses <code>selector<\/code>, and now every request to the workload fails. Why?", "a": "<p>A <code>selector<\/code>-based policy is enforced by ztunnel at L4, which can't parse HTTP paths. Rather than silently ignoring the L7 clause, Istio fails safe and turns it into a DENY. Move the L7 rule into an <code>AuthorizationPolicy<\/code> using <code>targetRefs<\/code> aimed at the Service or waypoint Gateway (making sure the target has <code>istio.io/use-waypoint<\/code> set), and keep any pure L4 identity check in ztunnel if needed.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#a-namespace-has-one-allow-policy-for-service-a-s", "sec": "5.5", "q": "A namespace has one ALLOW policy for service A. Service C, with no policy mentioning it, is suddenly refused by every caller. Explain.", "a": "<p>Deny-by-default kicks in for a workload the instant any <code>ALLOW<\/code> policy matches it. If that ALLOW policy omits <code>selector<\/code> or <code>targetRefs<\/code>, it applies to <em>every<\/em> workload in the namespace, unintentionally sweeping up service C and blocking everyone except A's allowed callers. Scope A's policy properly with a selector, or add an explicit ALLOW policy for C.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#in-linkerd-you-create-a-server-for-port-8080-and", "sec": "5.5", "q": "In Linkerd you create a <code>Server<\/code> for port 8080 and an <code>AuthorizationPolicy<\/code> allowing one client identity. Readiness probes start failing. Why, and what do you add?", "a": "<p>A <code>Server<\/code> defaults to <code>accessPolicy: deny<\/code>, and kubelet probes aren't in the mesh, so they're now rejected as unauthenticated traffic. Add an <code>HTTPRoute<\/code> matching the probe paths backed by an <code>AuthorizationPolicy<\/code> referencing a <code>NetworkAuthentication<\/code> for the node CIDRs (or <code>0.0.0.0/0<\/code>), or set <code>accessPolicy: all-unauthenticated<\/code> if it's a dedicated probe port.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.5#walk-the-spire-chain-from-a-pod-starts-to-it-hol", "sec": "5.5", "q": "Walk the SPIRE chain from \"a pod starts\" to \"it holds a certificate\": which component does what, and where do selectors come from?", "a": "<p>First, the node's <code>spire-agent<\/code> has already attested itself to <code>spire-server<\/code> (via <code>k8s_psat<\/code>). When the pod connects to the Workload API socket (mounted via <code>csi.spiffe.io<\/code>), the agent's <code>k8s<\/code> workload attestor asks the kubelet which pod owns that calling process and builds selectors (<code>k8s:ns<\/code>, <code>k8s:sa<\/code>, labels). The agent matches those selectors against registration entries under its parent ID (manual or generated by a <code>ClusterSPIFFEID<\/code>), gets <code>spire-server<\/code> to sign the cert, and streams the X509-SVID plus trust bundle back to the pod, automatically rotating before the 1h TTL expires.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#why-scan-in-the-pipeline-and-in-the-cluster", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Why scan in the pipeline <em>and<\/em> in the cluster?", "a": "<p>The pipeline gate blocks known vulnerabilities before an image ever hits the registry, but it can't predict CVEs disclosed tomorrow. Continuous in-cluster scanning catches those new CVEs on images already running. CI-only leaves you blind to everything newer than your last deploy; cluster-only lets vulnerable images ship in the first place.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#why-is-signing-by-digest-not-by-tag-the-point", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Why is signing by digest, not by tag, the point?", "a": "<p>A digest uniquely identifies exact image bytes, whereas a tag is just a mutable label. Signing the digest binds the signature to those specific bytes so nobody moving the tag can forge it. Verification resolves any tag to its digest first, and a hardened admission policy rewrites the pod spec to that pinned digest so runtime can never drift from what was checked.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#signatures-exist-and-someone-still-deployed-an-u", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Signatures exist and someone still deployed an unsigned image. What was missing?", "a": "<p>Admission-time verification. Without an enforcement point checking signatures at the door, signing is just decoration. Deploy an image-verification policy matching your registry glob in Deny mode, and double-check that its glob matches the exact in-cluster reference format pods actually use.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#kyverno-reports-no-signatures-found-for-an-image", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Kyverno reports \"no signatures found\" for an image you definitely signed. Three candidates?", "a": "<p>Check three usual suspects: registry reachability and hostname (Kyverno resolves from inside the cluster, so <code>localhost<\/code> won't reach your registry), HTTP vs HTTPS scheme (plain HTTP requires the engine's insecure-registry flag), and signature <em>format<\/em> skew (a cosign v3 bundle where the verifier expects the legacy <code>.sig<\/code> tag). Also check Rekor transparency-log settings if you signed with <code>--tlog-upload=false<\/code>.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#distinguish-sbom-provenance-and-signature-in-one", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Distinguish SBOM, provenance and signature in one sentence each.", "a": "<p><strong>SBOM:<\/strong> what components and versions are inside the artifact. <strong>Provenance:<\/strong> how, where, and from what source it was built (a SLSA-style attestation). <strong>Signature:<\/strong> who cryptographically vouches for these exact bytes. Together they form one trust story, and both SBOMs and provenance are typically stored as signed attestations.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#keyless-verification-which-two-flags-are-mandato", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Keyless verification: which two flags are mandatory, and what does each pin?", "a": "<p><code>--certificate-identity<\/code> (or <code>--certificate-identity-regexp<\/code>) pins the SAN inside the Fulcio cert (a signer's email, or a CI workflow URI like <code>https://github.com/org/repo/.github/workflows/build.yaml@refs/heads/main<\/code>). <code>--certificate-oidc-issuer<\/code> pins the OIDC provider that authenticated them (like <code>https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com<\/code>). Without both, any Fulcio-issued cert from anywhere would pass, which is why cosign refuses to run without them.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#tekton-chains-signs-the-taskrun-annotation-chain", "sec": "5.6", "q": "Tekton Chains signs the TaskRun (annotation <code>chains.tekton.dev/signed: \"true\"<\/code>) but <code>cosign verify-attestation<\/code> on the image finds nothing. Two configuration causes?", "a": "<p>Either the Task didn't emit type-hinted results (<code>IMAGE_URL<\/code> and <code>IMAGE_DIGEST<\/code>, or <code>*ARTIFACT_OUTPUTS<\/code>) so Chains didn't know an OCI image was produced, or <code>artifacts.taskrun.storage<\/code> / <code>artifacts.oci.storage<\/code> omitted <code>oci<\/code> so provenance only went to the <code>tekton<\/code> backend (run annotations). Inspect the TaskRun's results, the <code>chains-config<\/code> ConfigMap, and the Chains controller logs.<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#what-distinguishes-slsa-build-l2-from-l3-and-whi", "sec": "5.6", "q": "What distinguishes SLSA Build L2 from L3, and which one does a signed provenance from a shared CI runner reach?", "a": "<p>L2 requires a hosted build platform to generate and sign provenance using a platform-held key users can't touch. L3 goes further: builds must be isolated from each other and from user-controlled steps so a run can neither tamper with its own provenance nor access signing material. A shared runner where jobs can inspect each other's state or credentials tops out at L2; L3 requires ephemeral isolated builders with signing handled strictly outside the build step (like Tekton Chains signing from its own controller rather than inside the Task).<\/p>"},
{"id": "5.6#a-kyverno-imagevalidatingpolicy-fails-on-a-priva", "sec": "5.6", "q": "A Kyverno ImageValidatingPolicy fails on a private-registry image with an authentication error, although the pod's <code>imagePullSecrets<\/code> work. Why?", "a": "<p><code>ImageValidatingPolicy<\/code> doesn't read the pod's <code>imagePullSecrets<\/code> and ignores the global registry credential helper flag. You have to supply registry credentials explicitly on the policy itself via <code>spec.credentials.secrets<\/code> (Secrets in Kyverno's namespace) or <code>credentials.providers<\/code> for cloud ambient auth. Because Kyverno pulls signatures from its own pod, networking, TLS scheme (<code>allowInsecureRegistry<\/code>), and auth are all evaluated from Kyverno's side.<\/p>"}
];
